[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-3_Definitions
TITLE: Definition of terms, symbols and abbreviations

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Terms

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
For the purposes of the present document, the terms given in prEN 40000-1-1 [i.4] and the following apply:

.. note::
  NOTE: This clause provides terms and definitions based on CEN/CLC JTC13 WG09's [i.7] work on terms and definitions, terms and definitions provided by ETSI EN 303 645 [i.5] and by EN 18031 [i.6] series, and informed by terms used in the Common Criteria [i.8] and the NIAP Operating System Protection Plan [i.9] guide.

**administrator:** entity that is responsible for management activities, including setting policies that are applied by the enterprise on the operating system

.. note::
  NOTE: This administrator could be acting remotely through a management server, from which the system receives configuration policies. An administrator can enforce settings on the system which cannot be overridden by non-administrator users.

**application:** software that runs on a platform and performs tasks on behalf of the user or owner of the platform, as well as its supporting documentation

**Application Programming Interface (API):** specification of routines, data structures, object classes, and variables that allows an application to make use of services provided by another software component, such as a library

.. note::
  NOTE: APIs are often provided for a set of libraries included with the platform.

**attack surface:** user interfaces, target protocol interfaces and reachable data paths that can be attacked from inside or outside the system

**command shell:** text-based interface allowing execution of system programs

**Common Criteria (CC):** criteria for the evaluation of information technology security specified in ISO/IEC 15408 [i.8]

**Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE):** community-developed list of software and hardware weaknesses that can become vulnerabilities

**compensating control:** alternative control that meets the intent and rigour of a prescribed control when the prescribed control cannot be implemented for legitimate technical or business reasons

**core function:** function that is fundamental to the operation of the operating system, such as process scheduling, memory management, and input-output control, and whose loss of availability constitutes a denial-of-service condition

**credential:** data that establishes the identity of a user, e.g. a cryptographic key or password

**cryptographic mechanism:** security-related procedure using cryptography

.. note::
  NOTE 1: The term cryptographic mechanism is used as an umbrella term covering the categories used in the Agreed Cryptographic Mechanisms catalogue (ACM) [1], including cryptographic algorithms, primitives, schemes, protocols, protocol profiles, cipher suites, modes of operation, constructions and parameter sets.

.. note::
  NOTE 2: A cryptographic mechanism can be specified at different levels of abstraction. For example, AES is a cryptographic primitive, AES-GCM is a mode of operation and authenticated encryption construction, TLS 1.3 is a protocol, a TLS 1.3 restricted cipher-suite profile is a protocol profile, and TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 is a cipher suite.

**cryptographic property:** property provided or supported by a cryptographic mechanism

.. note::
  NOTE: Cryptographic properties include, for example, confidentiality, integrity, authenticity, entity authentication, message authentication, key establishment, key confirmation, replay protection, collision resistance, preimage resistance, signature unforgeability, non-repudiation, and forward secrecy. Where relevant, a cryptographic property can be expressed using a formal cryptographic notion, for example IND-CPA, IND-CCA2, EUF-CMA or SUF-CMA.

**data execution prevention:** anti-exploitation feature of modern operating systems executing on modern computer hardware, which enforces a non-execute permission on pages of memory that are not code

.. note::
  NOTE: This prevents pages of memory from containing both data and instructions, which makes it more difficult for an attacker to introduce and execute code.

**elevated privilege:** level of access that allows accessing or changing security-relevant configuration, data, or functions on a system

**essential function:** function whose availability is necessary for the product to fulfil its intended purpose

**general purpose operating system:** class of operating system designed to support a wide variety of workloads consisting of concurrent applications or services

.. note::
  NOTE: Typical characteristics of this category include support for third-party applications, support for multiple users, and security separation between users and their respective resources. General Purpose Operating Systems lack the operational constraints which define Special Purpose Operating Systems, including Real Time Operating System (RTOS), that are typically used in routers, switches, and embedded devices.

**Input/Output (I/O):** process or function for passing data to or from a given process over a specific interface

.. note::
  NOTE: Such I/O interfaces include, but are not limited to, serial ports, network ports, long-term storage devices including hard drives and flash drives, as well as human-interface ports such as display and audio devices.

**Operating System (OS):** Software products with digital elements that provide an abstract interface of the underlying hardware and control the execution of software, and that may provide services such as computing resource management and configuration, scheduling, input-output control, managing data, and providing an interface through which applications interact with system resources and peripherals. This category includes but is not limited to real-time operating systems, general-purpose and special-purpose operating systems.

.. note::
  NOTE: This definition is the technical description of the category of products with digital elements set out in Annex III, Class I, point 11 of Regulation (EU) 2024/2847 [i.1], as given in Commission Implementing Regulation (EU) 2025/2392 [i.2].

**Personally Identifiable Information (PII):** any information about an individual maintained by an agency, including but not limited to, education, financial transactions, medical history, and criminal or employment history and information which can be used to distinguish or trace an individual's identity

**principle of least privilege:** design principle requiring that users, processes, and interfaces are granted only the minimum level of permission necessary to perform their legitimate functions, and nothing more

**process isolation:** techniques to prevent processes from accessing or changing each other's state

**Remote Data Processing Solution (RDPS):** data processing at a distance for which the software is designed and developed by the manufacturer, or under the responsibility of the manufacturer, and the absence of which would prevent the product from performing one of its functions

.. note::
  NOTE: As defined in Regulation (EU) 2024/2847 [i.1], Article 3, point 2.

**residual risk:** risk remaining after risk treatment has been applied [i.10]

**security incident:** event that compromises the confidentiality, integrity, or availability of the product, of the data it stores, processes, or transmits, or of the services or functions it provides

**security profile:** set of baseline mitigations associated with a basic use case and assigned to a product on the basis of that use case

.. note::
  NOTE: A product implements the mitigations of its security profile together with the conditioned mitigations for each condition it meets, as specified in clause 5.3.

**sensitive data:** sensitive data may include all user or enterprise data or may be specific application data such as PII, emails, messaging, documents, calendar items, contacts, credentials, and keys

**system call interface:** specification for the API between the application layer and the kernel or system layer

**threat actor:** entity that can adversely affect the system through malicious or unauthorised activities

**user:** entity that is subject to configuration policies applied to the operating system by administrators

.. note::
  NOTE: On some systems under certain configurations, a normal user can temporarily elevate privileges to that of an administrator. At that time, such a user should be considered an administrator.

**user account:** identity created in an operating system with associated access controls and privileges

.. note::
  NOTE: Users may have multiple user accounts and user accounts may have multiple users.

**user data:** data entered or modified by the user, or generated by the user's use of the product, as opposed to data delivered with the product

.. note::
  NOTE: User data includes, for example, files stored by the user and credentials provisioned to the product, such as private keys, certificates, and pre-shared keys.
<<<

