<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-4_ProductContext
TITLE: Product context

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Intended purpose and reasonably foreseeable use

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The intended purpose of this product is to provide an abstract interface of the underlying hardware and control the execution of software, and potentially provide services such as computing resource management and configuration, scheduling, input-output control, managing data, and providing an interface through which applications interact with system resources and peripherals.

Its reasonably foreseeable use is to facilitate the use of computing hardware and serve as a platform for other software.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Product functions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The functions of an operating system commonly include process isolation, memory isolation, I/O abstraction, device driver management, user authentication and access control, event logging, software management, device firmware management, and secure updates.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Product architecture

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Overview

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
An operating system abstracts the hardware, allocates resources, and provides services to itself and any other software on the system. It often serves as a central organising authority that controls access to system resources by various pieces of software, dividing up available resources among the applications and its own subsystems to meet implicit or explicit goals or constraints. An operating system often uses a large part of the resources of the system it runs on, but in return it simplifies the development and deployment of the overall system.

Operating system architecture varies widely and depends on many factors, including the intended use case, the underlying platform, and the design philosophy of the developers. This overview will focus on the elements of operating system architecture that have a significant impact on the security functions and risk mitigations of an operating system.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Operating system security functions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Operating systems can vary in many ways which significantly affect security outcomes for the product. For this reason, the present document does not attempt to define specifics of operating system architecture. Some examples of differences include:

* the proportion of operating system code executed with different privileges;

* whether application code is executed with the same privileges as the operating system;

* the method of communication between processes with different privileges;

* the degree of reliance on hardware capabilities to enforce privilege separation and process isolation.

Each of these design variations makes different tradeoffs between security, performance, and ease of implementation.

The security of an operating system relies heavily on properly controlling the instructions executed by the system processor. Instruction flow is dependent on the program instructions, any data it accesses, and any hardware that has the ability to change either data or which instructions are being executed.

EXAMPLE 1: A network interface adapter can write directly to system memory, potentially altering data values and thereby influencing the behaviour of running programs or even causing unintended code execution.

EXAMPLE 2: A USB controller can raise an interrupt which can cause the interrupt controller to force the CPU to switch to executing interrupt handling code, altering the instruction flow.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Privileges

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
A fundamental building block of most operating systems is the principle of "privilege". Privilege determines which system resources or functions a program is allowed to access. The operating system grants a specific set of privileges to itself, its subsystems, and user-level programs, ensuring that each component operates within defined boundaries to maintain system security and stability.

Generally, privileges are enforced using hardware features such as a memory management unit and processor-defined privilege levels. If the hardware does not provide these features, the operating system can use a best effort approach, such as relying on the compiler to generate code that is less likely to accidentally interfere with the functions of the operating system or other parts of the system.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Access control mechanisms

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Operating systems may control access to resources in different ways, including but not limited to:

* Access control lists: Each resource has a list of users or processes that are allowed to access it.

* Role-based access control: Users are assigned one or more roles, and roles have permissions associated with them.

* Capabilities: Access to a resource is linked to a token which can be passed between processes.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Resource management

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Operating systems may control resource management in different ways, including but not limited to imposing per-process limitations on processor time, memory allocation, storage usage, number of file descriptors, or number of process table entries.

Operating systems may implement limits on the number or proportion of specific resources that an application or thread may use, and may group these limits by user, process, process group, or other mechanism.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Scheduling

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Operating systems may provide voluntary or involuntary switching between different processes, and may rely on hardware capabilities to improve or limit parallel process execution and isolation.

Some common models include:

Cooperative scheduling: Each thread runs until it voluntarily yields control of the processor to another thread. No thread is interrupted unless it explicitly yields the CPU.

Preemptive scheduling: Threads can be involuntarily suspended by the scheduler and replaced with other threads, and they may also voluntarily yield the CPU.

Operating systems may perform scheduling based on many factors, such as but not limited to:

* Time spent executing during a previous time period

* Time since the thread was first marked runnable

* Explicit priorities associated with each thread

* Type of thread (kernel or application)

* Resource limits

* Performance considerations
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Operational Environment

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The operational environment of an operating system is highly varied. In general it includes at least a platform (virtual or physical), and may also include applications, separately shipped device drivers, device firmware, peripherals, and many other components. Operating systems may be a standalone software product on a single processor, or it may be part of suite of software products running on multiple processors. Many systems have multiple operating systems all managing different parts of the system at the same time.

For the purposes of the use cases in clause 4.7, the following aspects of the operational environment are distinguished, as they determine which conditions in clause 4.7 hold for a product:

* the platform on which the operating system runs, whether virtual or physical;

* the network environment, namely whether the product, in foreseeable use, has unrestricted access to a public network or can be reached from one, accesses a public network only for restricted functions such as retrieving security updates, or has no access to a public network;

* the physical environment, namely whether the product is regularly exposed to untrusted persons, or is a portable device with a foreseeable likelihood of loss or theft; and

* the remote data-processing solutions on which one or more product functions depend, as described in clause 4.8.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Distribution of security functions

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
For each security requirement, a product can:

* Provide the necessary security function(s) itself

* Require security functions be provided by some other part of its context

* Provide security functions for the use of installed applications
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Security functions provided by some other part of its context

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Operating systems often rely on essential external functionality to implement their necessary security functionality.

For example, many operating systems rely on hardware secure elements and a compatible boot manager prior to the beginning of the operating system's own execution in order for the operating system to execute securely.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Security functions provided to other components

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Operating systems often provide essential security functions to other components of the system.

For example, most individual hardware components do not have a built-in method of secure firmware update, and rely on the presence of an operating system which can update firmware securely.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Users

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Users of products may interact directly with the operating system, or the operating system may be integrated into a product in such a way that the end user does not interact directly with any functionality of the operating system.

For the purposes of the use cases in clause 4.7, the following user categories are distinguished, as they determine which conditions in clause 4.7 hold for a product:

* end user, a person who uses the functions the product provides, without responsibility for its administration;

* administrator, a person responsible for configuring and maintaining the product; an administrator may be a professional administrator acting for the manufacturer or the operator, or a non-professional end user who administers their own product; and

* trusted and untrusted users, whether the users whose accounts the product hosts are mutually trusted; an operating system may host more than one end-user account, and may host concurrent accounts for users who are not mutually trusted.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Use cases

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This clause characterises the operating systems within the scope of the present document through use cases. A product is characterised by exactly one basic use case, as specified in clause 4.7.2, together with any conditions, as specified in clause 4.7.3, that the product meets. The use cases and conditions are described only by the characteristics relevant to threat identification, namely the user categories described in clause 4.6 and the operational environments described in clause 4.4.

The basic use case is determined by a single capability of the product: whether the product is a restricted operating system or a general-purpose computing platform. It fixes the baseline risk-factor levels of the product, as given in clause 4.7.5. Each applicable condition raises individual risk factors and determines the applicability of the requirements in clause 5. The use cases are neither exhaustive nor mutually exclusive.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Basic use cases

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-UC-REST
TITLE: UC-REST: Restricted operating system

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
An operating system that, as placed on the market, serves a restricted and bounded purpose and is not a general-purpose computing platform: it does not allow the end user to install and run third-party software without restriction, and it does not, by default, serve as an extensible platform for unrelated applications. Examples include operating systems integrated into appliances, sensors, and controllers. A restricted operating system begins from the lowest baseline risk-factor levels in clause 4.7.5; the capabilities and exposures it does have are added through the conditions of clause 4.7.3.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-UC-GEN
TITLE: UC-GEN: General-purpose operating system

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
An operating system that is a general-purpose computing platform, capable of running third-party software without restriction and of serving as a platform for unrelated applications. Examples include desktop, laptop, mobile, and server operating systems, and operating systems distributed as software for deployment on many kinds of device. A general-purpose product begins from the baseline risk-factor levels of a minimal single-user general-purpose system, as given in clause 4.7.5. Its further capabilities and exposures (network access, multiple accounts, installation of software, storage of sensitive data, administration by non-professional users, and the others in clause 4.7.3) are added through the conditions that hold for it.

Where the manufacturer cannot determine whether a condition holds for a general-purpose product, for example because the product is distributed as software whose deployment the manufacturer cannot constrain, the condition is assumed to hold. A general-purpose product for which every condition is assumed to hold is assessed against the most demanding profile.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Conditions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
A condition is a named property of a product that is associated with one or more risk factors. Where a condition holds for a product, it has both of the following effects:

* it raises each associated risk factor to at least the level stated in Table 4.7-1; and

* it determines the applicability of the requirements in clause 5 that carry the condition. A requirement that carries a condition applies only where that condition holds for the product.

The conditions are defined in Table 4.7-1.

.. list-table:: Table 4.7-1: Conditions
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 15 25 60

   * - Condition
     - Risk factors raised (minimum level)
     - Applicability condition
   * - C-NET
     - TNET-2, FNET-2, UEIN-2
     - This requirement applies where the product, in foreseeable use, has unrestricted access to a public network or can be reached from a public network.
   * - C-NETR
     - TNET-1
     - This requirement applies where the product accesses a public network only for restricted functions, such as retrieving security updates, and is not otherwise reachable from a public network.
   * - C-MOD
     - SWMD-1, HWMD-1, CONF-2
     - This requirement applies where foreseeable use permits the end user to modify security-relevant software, firmware, or hardware.
   * - C-APPS
     - SWMD-2
     - This requirement applies where the product facilitates the installation of third-party applications of uncertain provenance, for example through an application store or sideloading.
   * - C-LOSS
     - LOSS-2, PHYS-1
     - This requirement applies where the product is a portable device with a foreseeable likelihood of loss or theft.
   * - C-MULTI
     - NUSR-2
     - This requirement applies where the product hosts more than one end-user account.
   * - C-UNTR
     - CUSR-2
     - This requirement applies where the operating system hosts concurrent accounts for users who are not mutually trusted.
   * - C-DATA
     - SNDS-1, SNDT-1, PPII-1
     - This requirement applies where foreseeable use includes the processing, transmission, or short-term caching of sensitive or personal data.
   * - C-FUNC
     - SENF-2
     - This requirement applies where the product provides sensitive functions by default.
   * - C-RDPS
     - RDPS-1
     - This requirement applies where one or more product functions depend on a remote data-processing solution.
   * - C-PHYS
     - PHYS-2, DVCS-2
     - This requirement applies where foreseeable use includes regular physical exposure to untrusted persons.
   * - C-SELFADM
     - ADMN-2
     - This requirement applies where the product is administered by its own end users rather than by professional administrators.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Characterising a product

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
To characterise a product, the manufacturer shall:

1) identify the basic use case that corresponds to the product, namely UC-REST for a restricted operating system or UC-GEN for a general-purpose computing platform;

2) identify each condition in clause 4.7.3 that the product meets; where the product is characterised as UC-GEN and the manufacturer cannot determine whether a condition holds, the condition is treated as holding;

3) where the basic use case and one or more conditions assign different levels to the same risk factor, apply the highest of those levels; and

4) document the basis for each condition in clause 4.7.3 that is determined not to hold for the product.

The resulting set of risk-factor levels is the risk profile of the product. The product implements the security profile of its basic use case, defined in clause 5.3 (SP-REST for UC-REST, SP-GEN for UC-GEN), together with every mitigation carried by a condition that holds for the product. A mitigation that carries a condition applies only where that condition holds for the product.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Risk-factor levels of the basic use cases

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Table 4.7-2 gives the level that each basic use case assigns to each risk factor. The risk factors and their levels are defined in Annex B, where level 0 is the lowest and level 2 the highest. The conditions in clause 4.7.3 raise individual risk factors above these levels where the conditions hold.

.. list-table:: Table 4.7-2: Risk-factor levels of the basic use cases
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 60 20 20

   * - Risk factor
     - UC-REST
     - UC-GEN
   * - RF-NUSR Number of user accounts
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-CUSR User-account concurrency
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-PPII Collection of PII
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SNDS Sensitivity of data stored
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SNDT Sensitivity of data transmitted
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SENF Sensitivity of functions
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-PHYS Physical access by threat actors
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-UEIN Untrusted external inputs
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-LOSS Probability of loss/theft
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-HWMD Hardware modifiability
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SWMD Software modifiability
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-DVCS Untrusted peripheral devices
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-TNET Access to a public network
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-FNET Reached from untrusted networks
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-CONF Configurability
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-ADMN Administration
     - 2
     - 1
   * - RF-SUPP Support and foreseeable updates
     - 1
     - 2
   * - RF-RDPS Remote data-processing dependency
     - 0
     - 0
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Remote data-processing dependencies

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This clause identifies the remote data-processing solutions within the scope of the present document, the interfaces through which the product communicates with them, and the product functions that depend on them. Where a product function depends on a remote data-processing solution, condition C-RDPS as specified in clause 4.7.3 holds, and the requirements in clause 5 that carry that condition apply. The scope of the present document is limited to the boundary between the product and the remote data-processing solution, namely the product-side endpoint and the interactions exchanged across that boundary. The present document does not extend the scope of the product to the remote service environment or to the underlying third-party infrastructure.

The remote data-processing solution is assumed to be subject to the security controls applied by its operator. The trust relationship between the product and the remote data-processing solution is assumed to be established at product initialisation through credentials or trust anchors provisioned by the manufacturer or the integrator.

.. note::
  NOTE: The requirements of the present document apply to the product side of the boundary. Where the manufacturer also controls the solution side, the manufacturer applies equivalent protection to the interactions received and acted upon on that side.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Remote data-processing solutions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
A remote data-processing solution within the scope of the present document is a service operated by, or under the responsibility of, the manufacturer, the absence of which would prevent the product from performing one or more of its functions.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: RDPS interfaces

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The interface between the product and a remote data-processing solution is the communication boundary between the product-side endpoint, namely the client, agent, or connector running on the product, and the solution-side endpoint exposed by the remote data-processing solution. This interface typically traverses a public network that is not under the control of the user of the product. Communication across this interface is assumed to be conducted over a secure channel and to be authenticated at the endpoint level.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: RDPS-dependent product functions

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Product functions that may depend on a remote data-processing solution can include, but are not limited to, the following:

* cloud-backed authentication and account management, where the product authenticates the end user against a manufacturer-operated service;

* telemetry, attestation, and health reporting from the product to a manufacturer-operated service; and

* device management, where the product receives configuration or security-relevant commands from a manufacturer-operated service.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-5_Requirements
TITLE: Technical requirements for products

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Notes on the structure of the Requirements

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The technical cybersecurity requirements in clause 5.2 are organised as a set of technical requirements, each identified as TR-XXX. A technical requirement groups the mitigations, each identified as MI-XXX, that address one security objective for the product, and is satisfied by implementing them. A mitigation is a specific, verifiable technical means of meeting that objective, and states the assessment objective against which it is verified. Each mitigation is stated together with the assessment activity that verifies it (clause 6) and, where it relies on a product feature that not all operating system architectures provide, an applicability field that limits it to products with that feature.

A product does not necessarily implement every mitigation. The mitigations that a given product shall implement are those of its security profile, together with those carried by the conditions the product meets, as specified in clause 5.3. The security profiles and conditions are derived from the risk analysis in Annex B, so that the mitigations required of a product are proportionate to its risk.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Necessity of Requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Not all requirements are necessary for all products. The set of mitigations necessary for a given product is determined by its security profile and the conditions it meets, as specified in clause 5.3, and derived from the risk analysis in Annex B. In addition, a mitigation that carries an applicability field, stated with the mitigation, applies only where the product has the feature that the field identifies.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Types of Technical Requirements

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
All technical requirements in the present document specify properties of the product or obligations of the manufacturer that are verifiable. Verification is performed through the assessment activities defined in clause 6, each corresponding to a mitigation in clause 5.2.

Where a product property can be verified by testing the product directly, the assessment activity is a test procedure with a measurable verdict.

Where a product property cannot be verified by testing the product directly, the assessment activity is a documentation review. In this case the manufacturer shall provide supporting documentation as conformity assessment evidence. The documentation does not replace the product property. It demonstrates that the property holds.

.. note::
  NOTE: Simple process statements (e.g. a statement that something has been done, without supporting evidence) are not acceptable as conformity assessment evidence.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Assumptions Regarding Requirements

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Testability

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Manufacturers are already required to provide the ability to enable testing and collect output on the product as placed on the market, and will supply instructions for enabling and collecting test data.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Mitigations

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Mitigations are the technical means by which a technical requirement is satisfied. Each mitigation's applicability to a given product is determined by the risk factors and security profile mappings in clause 5.3 and Annex B.

Not all mitigations are necessary for all products. See clause 5.3 for the mapping of security profiles and conditions to mitigations, and Annex B for additional information.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Residual risk

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Some cybersecurity risks cannot be fully eliminated by the product alone and result in residual risk after the product's mitigations have been applied. Where this is the case, the manufacturer shall document the residual risk and the actions the user or the operational environment can take to address it.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Technical security requirements specifications

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This clause specifies the technical cybersecurity requirements for operating systems. Each technical requirement (TR-XXX) is satisfied by implementing the mitigations (MI-XXX) listed under it. The set of mitigations that a given product shall implement is determined by the security profile assigned to that product in clause 5.3: every baseline mitigation of that profile, together with every conditioned mitigation in clause 5.3.4 whose condition the product meets. Where a mitigation also carries an applicability field stated with its requirement, that mitigation applies only when the stated feature condition holds for the product. Compliance with a technical requirement is demonstrated by passing the assessment activities defined for each applicable mitigation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-NKEV
TITLE: TR-NKEV: No known exploitable vulnerabilities

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVM
TITLE: MI-KEVM: Documentation of mitigation of known exploitable vulnerabilities
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-KEVM]

The product shall be accompanied by documentation demonstrating that all known exploitable vulnerabilities have been identified and either remediated or documented with the residual risk and any compensating control. The manufacturer shall consider public vulnerability databases (such as CVE, NVD) and upstream security advisories from component maintainers in addition to the manufacturer's own vulnerability research. Reported issues shall be triaged according to the manufacturer's documented secure development and vulnerability remediation policy, including severity classification, exploitability assessment, and compensating controls. The product is deemed to be compliant with this requirement if it:

* has no known exploitable vulnerabilities; or

* has known exploitable vulnerabilities that were disclosed within the manufacturer's documented vulnerability remediation timeline and for which assessment and remediation are in progress; and

* for each remaining vulnerability, has documentation of the residual risk and any compensating control.

.. note::
  NOTE: Publicly reported severity ratings and reports of known active exploitation are an input to, not a substitute for, the manufacturer's own assessment of a vulnerability's exploitability and impact in the context of the product. Where this product-specific assessment of the risk level differs from the publicly reported level, the manufacturer's assessment governs prioritisation, with the justification documented.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-NKEV

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVT
TITLE: MI-KEVT: Testing for known exploitable vulnerabilities
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-KEVT]

Before release, the product shall undergo verification that all known exploitable vulnerabilities have been mitigated. Verification may use manual testing, automated scanning, or both. The manufacturer shall consider public vulnerability databases (such as CVE, NVD) and upstream security advisories from component maintainers in addition to the manufacturer's own vulnerability research. Reported issues shall be triaged according to the manufacturer's documented secure development and vulnerability remediation policy, including severity classification, exploitability assessment, and compensating controls. The product is deemed to be compliant with this requirement if it:

* has no known exploitable vulnerabilities; or

* has known exploitable vulnerabilities that were disclosed within the manufacturer's documented vulnerability remediation timeline and for which assessment and remediation are in progress; and

* for each tested vulnerability:

  * the test result shows that the vulnerability has been mitigated; or
  * the tested vulnerability has documentation of the residual risk and any compensating control.

.. note::
  NOTE: Publicly reported severity ratings and reports of known active exploitation are an input to, not a substitute for, the manufacturer's own assessment of a vulnerability's exploitability and impact in the context of the product. Where this product-specific assessment of the risk level differs from the publicly reported level, the manufacturer's assessment governs prioritisation, with the justification documented.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-NKEV

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-MINI
TITLE: TR-MINI: Minimise impact on other devices and services

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RRIS
TITLE: MI-RRIS: Document residual risk to other devices and services
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RRIS]

The product shall be accompanied by documentation describing the residual risk to other devices and services on the network and the actions the user or the operational environment can take to mitigate it.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-MINI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-MNET
TITLE: MI-MNET: Minimise negative impact of network transmission
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-MNET]

The product shall provide mechanisms that limit the rate and volume of network traffic it transmits so as to minimise impact on other products or services. The details of these mechanisms shall be documented, including the default configuration and how this contributes to minimising potential interference with other products or services on the network.

.. note::
  NOTE: Mechanisms include but are not limited to connection limits (per-client and total limits), retry/backoff strategies, rate limiting of unicast, broadcast or multicast traffic including per-connection limits, traffic shaping or traffic policing.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-MINI

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-SDEF
TITLE: TR-SDEF: Secure by default configuration

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ADEF
TITLE: MI-ADEF: Authorisation required by default to access security-relevant assets
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ADEF]

The product, in its default state, shall require authentication and authorisation for access to security-relevant assets. The authentication mechanism and the authorisation rules applied to each class of security-relevant asset shall be documented by the manufacturer. For example, an autogenerated device-specific cryptographic key should not be readable without authentication and authorisation. Where the product is intended for integration into another product, the manufacturer shall document any security-relevant assets for which authentication and authorisation are not enforced by the product itself and are expected to be provided by the integrator.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SDEF

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-PDDI-1
TITLE: MI-PDDI-1: Document how to control access to debug and management interfaces
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-PDDI-1]

All debug and management interfaces on the product shall be documented, and the documentation shall specify means to control access to or disable them.

* Applicability: This mitigation is for products intended for integration into subsequent products
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SDEF

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-PDDI-2
TITLE: MI-PDDI-2: Control local software access to debug and management interfaces
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-PDDI-2]

All debug and management interfaces which can be accessed by processes running on the system shall be access controlled or disabled by default, unless required for backward compatibility. Where the product allows the user to remove such protections, the manufacturer shall document the procedure, the residual risk, and the user roles permitted to perform the procedure.

.. note::
  NOTE: On general-purpose operating systems, device unlock and explicit authorised user consent is recognised as sufficient authorisation to enable these interfaces, without requiring predefined professional administrator roles.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SDEF

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-PDDI-3
TITLE: MI-PDDI-3: Control network access to debug or management interfaces
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-PDDI-3]

All debug and management interfaces accessible via the network shall be access controlled or disabled by default, unless necessary for backward compatibility. Documentation regarding the removal of such protections by an appropriately sophisticated user may be provided, and shall include information regarding the risks.

* Applicability: Product exposes debug or management interfaces accessible via the network
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SDEF

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD
TITLE: TR-SCUD: Secure updates

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVD
TITLE: MI-KEVD: Documentation for secure update before or during first use
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-KEVD]

The product shall be accompanied by documentation describing how the product may be securely updated, including how to update the product prior to, or as part of, first use.

* Applicability: The product has firmware or software update capability
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVA
TITLE: MI-KEVA: Secure update before or during first use
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-KEVA]

The product shall implement a secure update mechanism that operates by default, including before or during first use.

The product shall provide a clear and easy-to-use mechanism allowing an authorised user to opt out of automatic security updates.

If an authorised user opted out of automatic security updates, the product shall provide, before or during first use, a mechanism to inform the user to initiate or postpone the update.

* Applicability: The product has firmware or software update capability
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SCUE
TITLE: MI-SCUE: Security updates provided by the operational environment
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SCUE]

The product shall be capable of receiving and applying secure updates provided by the operational environment, with their authenticity and integrity verified in accordance with MI-SUVI. The manufacturer shall document the requirements that the operational environment shall satisfy to deliver secure updates.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SUVI
TITLE: MI-SUVI: Update authenticity and integrity verification
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SUVI]

The product shall verify the authenticity and integrity of update packages before installation. For a given update path, this verification may be satisfied by any one of the following mechanisms:

* verification of a cryptographic digital signature on the update package by the product;

* verification of update integrity only, by a hash or checksum, where authenticity is assured by compensating controls such as restricted access control, an authenticated delivery channel, or controlled tooling;

* an authenticated secure channel between the product and an authorised update source;

* controlled local or offline update through authorised personnel, trusted tools, and controlled update media; or

* a trusted platform component, such as a package manager or installer, that verifies the authenticity and integrity of update packages and controls their installation.

Where cryptographic mechanisms are used, they shall conform to TR-CRYP.

.. note::
  NOTE: For image-based operating systems, installation means activating the new image.

* Applicability: The product has firmware or software update capability
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SUDP
TITLE: MI-SUDP: Anti-rollback protection
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SUDP]

The product shall maintain a monotonic version counter or an equivalent mechanism to prevent the unauthorised installation of an earlier software version.

* Applicability: The product has firmware or software update capability
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SUAR
TITLE: MI-SUAR: Authorised rollback
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SUAR]

Where the product supports intentional, user-initiated rollback to an earlier software version, the rollback shall require explicit authorisation. Automated recovery mechanisms that revert to the immediately preceding operational state following a failed update or boot failure are considered recovery actions and are exempt from this explicit authorisation requirement.

* Applicability: The product supports intentional, user-initiated rollback to an earlier software version
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCUD

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH
TITLE: TR-AUTH: Authentication and access control

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RAUT
TITLE: MI-RAUT: Authenticate the remote data processing solution and validate its interactions at the RDPS boundary
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RAUT]

Where the product communicates with a remote data processing solution, the product shall authenticate the remote data processing solution before transmitting data to it. Before acting upon an interaction received from a remote data processing solution that can influence the behaviour, state, configuration, or security of a product function, the product shall verify the authenticity and integrity of that interaction and shall reject replayed or stale interactions where replay or reuse could affect the secure operation of the product function. The required verification may be satisfied by the properties of a secure channel conforming to TR-CRYP.

* Applicability: Product depends on one or more remote data processing solutions
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-AUTH-1
TITLE: MI-AUTH-1: Require user authentication on interfaces providing access to the operating system
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-AUTH-1]

The operating system shall require user authentication by default on all interfaces that provide access to operating system resources or functions that are not intended for unauthenticated use.

* Applicability: Product provides an interface that supports user or administrator authentication
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-AUTH-2
TITLE: MI-AUTH-2: Require privilege or authorisation on interfaces providing access to the operating system
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-AUTH-2]

The operating system shall ensure that interfaces providing access to operating system resources or functions not intended for unauthenticated use cannot be invoked by an unauthorised account. Access shall require the account to hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation, whether presented by an interactively authenticated administrator or by an authorised service or machine identity.

* Applicability: Product provides an interface that supports user or administrator authentication
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-LCKT
TITLE: MI-LCKT: Authentication failure protection
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-LCKT]

The operating system shall by default enforce authentication failure protection on all authentication interfaces.

.. note::
  NOTE: Examples of enforcement mechanisms include progressive delays between failed attempts or temporary account lockout after a number of failed attempts.

* Applicability: Product provides an interface that supports user or administrator authentication
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-CRED
TITLE: MI-CRED: Protect stored critical security parameters and enforce credential strength
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-CRED]

The operating system shall protect stored credentials, cryptographic keys, and other critical security parameters using mechanisms conforming to TR-CRYP, and shall by default enforce minimum credential strength requirements documented by the manufacturer for credentials it accepts through authentication interfaces.

* Applicability: Product stores credentials, cryptographic keys, or other critical security parameters, or accepts credentials through a user or administrator authentication interface
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ACCS
TITLE: MI-ACCS: Access control enforcement on operating system resources and functions
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ACCS]

The operating system shall enforce access control on operating system resources and functions based on the authenticated identity, role, privilege level, or other attributes of the requesting subject, as documented by the manufacturer.

* Applicability: Product supports differentiated access to operating system resources or functions among subjects
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-PRIV
TITLE: MI-PRIV: Privilege separation and restriction
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-PRIV]

Where the operating system supports multiple privilege levels, the operating system shall enforce privilege separation between subjects executing at different privilege levels, and shall restrict each subject to the operations permitted at its assigned privilege level and deny privilege escalation attempts within an active session without re-authentication or other documented authorisation.

* Applicability: Product supports multiple privilege levels
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SESS
TITLE: MI-SESS: Interactive session lifecycle controls
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SESS]

Where the operating system supports authenticated interactive sessions that persist after initial authentication, the operating system shall invalidate the interactive session upon session logout or termination, with any security context or tokens derived from that session continuing only according to the product's documented session model.

* Applicability: Product supports authenticated interactive sessions that persist after initial authentication
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ACSD
TITLE: MI-ACSD: Access control for stored data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ACSD]

The product shall protect stored data from unauthorised access or modification by enforcing access controls and privilege separation at the operating system level.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AUTH

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-CDST
TITLE: TR-CDST: Confidentiality of data stored on the product

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ENST
TITLE: MI-ENST: Cryptographic encryption of stored data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ENST]

Where the product stores user data, the product shall, by default, provide the option to protect the confidentiality of that data at rest using a cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

* Applicability: Product stores user data
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-CDST

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-CDTX
TITLE: TR-CDTX: Confidentiality of data transmitted by the product

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-CDTX
TITLE: MI-CDTX: Protect confidentiality of user data transmitted by the product
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-CDTX]

The product shall, by default, protect user data transmitted by the product from unauthorised access.

.. note::
  NOTE: This only applies where the product initiates the data processing leading to transmission, and does not apply to user initiated actions while using the product.

* Applicability: Product transmits user data
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-CDTX

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RRDC
TITLE: MI-RRDC: Document residual risk to confidentiality of data transmitted
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RRDC]

Where the product transmits user data, the product shall be accompanied by documentation describing the residual risk to the confidentiality of that data transmitted by the product and the actions the user or the operational environment can take to mitigate it.

.. note::
  NOTE: This only applies where the product initiates the data processing leading to transmission, and does not apply to user initiated actions while using the product.

* Applicability: Product transmits user data
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-CDTX

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ENTX
TITLE: MI-ENTX: Cryptographic encryption of transmitted data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ENTX]

Where the product transmits user data, the product shall, by default, protect the confidentiality of that data in transit using a cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

.. note::
  NOTE: This only applies where the product initiates the data processing leading to transmission, and does not apply to user initiated actions while using the product.

* Applicability: Product transmits user data
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-CDTX

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-CRYP
TITLE: TR-CRYP: Encryption

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-CRYP
TITLE: MI-CRYP: Conformance to Annex K
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-CRYP]

The product shall conform to Annex K.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-CRYP

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-IDST
TITLE: TR-IDST: Integrity of data stored on the product

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-DCST
TITLE: MI-DCST: Detect corruption of stored data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-DCST]

The product shall provide the capability to detect corruption of stored data.

.. note::
  NOTE 1: Mechanisms include but are not limited to checksums, parity, or error-correcting codes.

.. note::
  NOTE 2: The availability of the capability of the product in its operational environment might be dependent on user-choice of configuration such as the file system.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-IDST

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-INTS
TITLE: MI-INTS: Cryptographic integrity protection of stored data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-INTS]

Where the product uses cryptographic mechanisms for integrity protection of stored data, they shall conform to TR-CRYP, such as message authentication codes or digital signatures.

* Applicability: Product uses cryptographic mechanisms for integrity protection of stored data
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-IDST

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-IDTX
TITLE: TR-IDTX: Integrity of data transmitted by the product

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-INTT
TITLE: MI-INTT: Cryptographic integrity protection of transmitted data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-INTT]

Where the product transmits user data, the product shall, by default, protect the integrity of that data in transit using a cryptographic integrity mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP, such as a message authentication code or a digital signature.

.. note::
  NOTE: This only applies where the product initiates the data processing leading to transmission, and does not apply to user initiated actions while using the product.

* Applicability: Product transmits user data
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-IDTX

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-DMIN
TITLE: TR-DMIN: Data Minimisation

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-DJST
TITLE: MI-DJST: Document and justify processed data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-DJST]

All sources of user data processed by the product in its secure-by-default configuration shall be documented. All sources of user data processed shall have a documented rationale for why its processing is necessary for the functioning of the product in its secure-by-default configuration.

.. note::
  NOTE: This only applies where the product initiates the data processing leading to transmission, and does not apply to user initiated actions while using the product.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-DMIN

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI
TITLE: TR-AVAI: Availability

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-AVNT
TITLE: MI-AVNT: Availability of network functions
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-AVNT]

The product shall protect the availability of essential and core network functions through mitigation of denial-of-service attacks.

.. note::
  NOTE 1: Denial-of-service protection mechanisms may include TCP SYN flooding attack mitigations as specified in IETF RFC 4987 [i.11].

.. note::
  NOTE 2: Mechanisms to drop undesirable or excessive network packets before incurring significant resource usage associated with processing those packets can include SYN cookies, packet filtering before connection tracking, configurable timeouts for connections, and limits on the number of simultaneous connections.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-CSFR
TITLE: MI-CSFR: Detection and recovery from critical service failures
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-CSFR]

The product shall implement mechanisms to detect and recover from failures of the services the manufacturer documents as critical services.

.. note::
  NOTE: For general-purpose operating systems, this could include mechanisms such as automatically restarting failed essential services, handling critical kernel faults, or providing a 'safe mode' recovery environment. Where appropriate for the use case (such as embedded or real-time operating systems) and supported by the hardware, the product may utilise a hardware watchdog to trigger a reset.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RLIM
TITLE: MI-RLIM: Resource usage limits and exhaustion fallback
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RLIM]

The product shall provide configurable mechanisms that allow authorised users to constrain memory and resource usage for processes and network connections.

The product shall implement a fallback mechanism that constrains the processes or resource consumers the manufacturer documents as subject to the fallback, so as to preserve the availability of the functions the manufacturer documents as essential or core functions, when documented resource-exhaustion thresholds are reached.

Where the product exposes shared or limited resources to multiple concurrent users, these mechanisms shall also be capable of preventing a user from causing sustained denial of service to other users through resource exhaustion.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-PRIO
TITLE: MI-PRIO: Workload prioritisation
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-PRIO]

The product shall implement scheduling capabilities that support priority levels for processes and network traffic. The product shall be able to prioritise the functions the manufacturer documents as core operating system functions over lower-priority workloads when documented resource-contention thresholds are reached.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RRDS
TITLE: MI-RRDS: Document residual risk of denial of service
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RRDS]

The product shall be accompanied by documentation describing the residual risk of denial of service and the measures the operational environment can provide to mitigate it, such as an external or internal firewall, fair queueing or filtering, or a proxy.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RTDL
TITLE: MI-RTDL: Real-time deadline preservation
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RTDL]

The product shall demonstrate that its security mechanisms do not cause the product to exceed documented worst-case execution times for real-time tasks.

* Applicability: Product has documented real-time deadlines
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RTRY
TITLE: MI-RTRY: Retry and degraded behaviour on unavailability of the remote data processing solution
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RTRY]

Where the availability or timeliness of interactions with a remote data processing solution is necessary for the secure operation of the product, the product shall detect unavailability of the remote data processing solution or unacceptable delay of expected interactions, apply defined timeout controls for expected interactions, retry failed communications within a manufacturer-documented retry window, justified in the product risk assessment and appropriate to the dependent product function, and apply a defined behaviour for the dependent product function when the remote data processing solution remains unavailable beyond the acceptable time.

* Applicability: Product depends on one or more remote data processing solutions
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-AVAI

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL
TITLE: TR-ISOL: Isolation

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-1
TITLE: MI-ISO-1: Access control across security boundaries
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-1]

The product shall enforce security boundaries that restrict access to security-relevant assets to authorised subjects and authorised operations. Where the product supports multiple users, applications, processes, execution contexts, privilege levels, or administrative roles, the product shall provide mechanisms that distinguish those subjects and restrict access across security boundaries according to the product's access-control and isolation model.

.. note::
  NOTE: This mitigation states, as a general security-boundary requirement, the access-control and privilege principles that MI-ACCS and MI-PRIV enforce under TR-AUTH.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-2
TITLE: MI-ISO-2: Process isolation
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-2]

The product shall enforce isolation between processes at the CPU, memory, I/O, and interrupt-handling levels, ensuring that one process cannot read or modify the resources of another process, and cannot degrade the availability of another process beyond what is permitted by the product's configured scheduling and resource-sharing policies.

.. note::
  NOTE: Performance variability caused by normal scheduling and resource sharing, for example reduced CPU time under load, is acceptable when it remains within the product's configured scheduling and resource-sharing policies.

* Applicability: Product executes more than one process concurrently
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-3
TITLE: MI-ISO-3: Isolation between the processes of different users
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-3]

The product shall enforce isolation between processes belonging to different users, ensuring that one user cannot read or modify the resources of another user, and cannot degrade the availability of another user's processes beyond what is permitted by the product's configured scheduling and resource-sharing policies.

* Applicability: Product hosts more than one user account
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-4
TITLE: MI-ISO-4: Isolation between the data of different users
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-4]

The product shall by default enforce isolation between data belonging to different users, ensuring that one user cannot read or modify the data of another user, and cannot degrade the availability of another user's data beyond what is permitted by the product's configured resource-sharing policies.

* Applicability: Product hosts more than one user account
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-5
TITLE: MI-ISO-5: Privilege model
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-5]

The product shall provide a privilege model that provides different levels of access to system resources requiring separate authorisation according to the product's access-control and isolation model.

.. note::
  NOTE: This mitigation complements MI-PRIV under TR-AUTH, which enforces privilege separation between subjects executing at different privilege levels.

* Applicability: Product is a general-purpose operating system
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-6
TITLE: MI-ISO-6: Side-channel isolation
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-6]

The product shall by default strengthen isolation with mitigations against known side-channel attacks between workloads sharing hardware resources, as documented by the product's risk assessment and relevant to the known attack classes applicable to the product and its supported hardware.

The product documentation shall include the mitigation's default state and rationale, and the residual risks.

The product shall enable, by default, the mitigations for the attack classes whose risk is present across the product's intended deployment scenarios.

Where the risk assessment shows that a class of side-channel attacks is deployment-specific and that enabling its mitigation would impose a disproportionate impact on the scenarios in which the risk is low or absent, the product may provide the mitigation off by default and include the process by which an authorised user can activate the mitigation.

Where effective mitigation depends on platform mechanisms outside the declared product boundary or outside the responsibility of the manufacturer, those mechanisms shall be treated as Operational Environment dependencies. The product security documentation shall identify the dependency and the expected security properties.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-ISO-7
TITLE: MI-ISO-7: Binary hardening
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-ISO-7]

The product binaries shall exhibit the hardening measures documented by the manufacturer for the target platform and the respective language or languages for the respective product binaries. The manufacturer shall document the risk-appropriate hardening measures and the rationale applied to each product binary and to groups of binaries, together with the residual risk and any compensating control.

.. note::
  NOTE: Example hardening measures depend on the programming language, the CPU architecture, and the CPU features. Non-memory-safe languages may use techniques such as heap corruption detection, buffer overflow detection, stack overflow protection, stack clash protection, non-executable heap, non-executable stack, and position-independent executables. Memory-safe languages may use the compiler itself for protection and a reduction in the number of unsafe constructs. Generic hardening measures include address space layout randomisation, control flow integrity, memory tagging, and mathematical proofs.

* Applicability: Product is implemented in a compiled language
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-ISOL

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-LOGG
TITLE: TR-LOGG: Logging and monitoring

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-LOGG
TITLE: MI-LOGG: Logging
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-LOGG]

The product shall record time-stamped log messages for security-relevant events in accordance with the manufacturer's default secure configuration. Each log record shall include, as applicable to the event type, the time of the event, the outcome of the event, and the identity of the associated user, service, or process. The manufacturer shall document, based on its risk assessment, which events are treated as security-relevant for the product.

Log records produced in the product's default secure configuration shall not include plaintext credentials or full cryptographic keys. Log, telemetry, and diagnostic data shall be limited to that necessary for operation, troubleshooting, and security monitoring. Where the product collects data that may contain sensitive content, the manufacturer shall document that collection.

The product shall provide an authorised user with a mechanism to opt-out of the recording and monitoring of security-relevant events.

.. note::
  NOTE 1: Security-relevant events can include, but are not limited to, access or modification of system data, services or functions.

.. note::
  NOTE 2: Sensitive log content can include, but is not limited to, memory or crash dumps, register or pointer state, command-line or script capture, or debug payloads.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-LOGG

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-SCDL
TITLE: TR-SCDL: Secure deletion

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SDEL
TITLE: MI-SDEL: Secure deletion
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SDEL]

The product shall provide a means by which the user can securely delete user data and settings stored on the product, such that the deleted data and settings cannot subsequently be recovered through the product's interfaces.

.. note::
  NOTE: The means may be a reset to the secure-by-default state, a reinstallation, or a secure deletion function. For products employing hardware-backed storage encryption (conforming to TR-CRYP), cryptographic erasure (destruction of the encryption key) is recognised as a sufficient method of secure deletion regardless of data sensitivity.

* Applicability: Product has the capability for the user to write data and/or settings
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCDL

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-RRSD
TITLE: MI-RRSD: Document residual risk to secure deletion
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-RRSD]

The product shall be accompanied by documentation describing the residual risk to secure deletion, including any user data or settings that the product cannot guarantee to delete, and the actions the user or the operational environment can take to mitigate it.

* Applicability: Product has the capability for the user to write data and/or settings
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SCDL

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TR-SDTR
TITLE: TR-SDTR: Secure data read and transfer

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SDRF
TITLE: MI-SDRF: Secure data read from product
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SDRF]

The product shall provide a method by which an authorised user can securely read their data and settings from the product. Reading system data or data shared by multiple users may be limited to an authorised user having sufficient privileges.

* Applicability: Product has the capability for the user to write data and/or settings
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-TR-SDTR

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-MI-SDTR
TITLE: MI-SDTR: Document secure data transfer to another product or system
DESCRIPTION: >>>
Assessment criteria: [AC-SDTR]

Where the product provides a method to transfer data and settings to another product or system, it shall be accompanied by documentation describing how the confidentiality and integrity of the data and settings are protected during the transfer.

* Applicability: Product has the capability for the user to write data and/or settings and to transfer them to another product or system
<<<
