[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-6_AssessmentCriteria
TITLE: Assessment criteria for compliance with technical requirements

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: Introduction to the assessment and compliance criteria

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This clause details the assessment process for compliance with the requirements in clause 5 of the present document. Each assessment activity in this clause corresponds to a mitigation in clause 5.2.

.. note::
  NOTE: In this clause, documentation means any documentation provided to the assessor. This includes, but is not limited to, the technical specification, test results, and instructions to the user.

This clause provides objective and reproducible assessment criteria to determine whether a product complies with the technical security requirements of clause 5.

For each cybersecurity requirements defined in clause 5, the following clauses specify assessment criteria to determine if the technical requirement is met.

The assessment criteria for each security requirements are described in a structured manner, as follows:

* Assessment reference: Refers to the identifier of the concerned technical requirement.

* Assessment objective: Defines the security property or capability that shall be verified, ensuring that the assessment remains focused on the intent of the requirement.

* Assessment preparation: Describes the environment, setup, and preconditions required before executing the test with a view to guaranteeing consistent evaluation results. It includes the following elements as applicable:

  * Test environment: Describe the hardware, software, and network setup used for the assessment, including versions, topology, and any relevant dependencies.
  * Preconditions: Specify any configurations, credentials, or operational states that should be established before the test (e.g. product initialised, certificates loaded, user roles created).
  * Required tools: Identify the tools or software necessary to perform the assessment (e.g. vulnerability scanners, protocol fuzzers, traffic analysers, static code analysers, cryptographic test suites).
  * Required information/documentation for the assessment: Specify all information that is necessary to perform the assessment.
  * Reference any vendor-provided setup guides, configuration instructions, or operational manuals, as well as any relevant standards or technical notes, that define how the product shall be configured or operated for the assessment.

* Assessment activities: Provides execution steps to be performed such that the same assessment verdict would be reached when repeating these steps now or at a later point in time. Assessment activities may include, as applicable:

  * Review information/documentation for the assessment to confirm that the described implementation matches the requirement (e.g. verify that the security architecture document specifies TLS 1.2 or higher for all external interfaces, or that the password policy aligns with the defined threshold).
  * Perform security functional tests to verify the completeness and correctness of the information/documentation for the assessment.
  * Perform security functional or penetration tests to verify that implemented controls are correctly implemented e.g. to prevent unauthorised access or data modification (e.g. via attempting to log in with invalid credentials to test lockout enforcement or trying to modify protected configuration files without administrative privileges).
  * Analyse code or binaries to identify potential security weaknesses or misconfigurations (e.g. perform static analysis to detect hardcoded credentials or use dynamic analysis tools to identify buffer overflow or injection vulnerabilities).
  * Inspect configurations to ensure that required security parameters are correctly applied (e.g. check that weak cipher suites are disabled, two-factor authentication is enabled, and least-privilege access controls are configured in the system).
  * Observe runtime behaviour to confirm that protections such as encryption, authentication, and integrity verification operate as intended (e.g. monitor network traffic to ensure data in transit is encrypted or observe system logs to verify successful validation of digital signatures during startup).

* Assessment verdict: Defines the pass/fail criteria.

  * Pass: The assessment is considered passed if the product demonstrably fulfils the requirement and meets the defined security thresholds. Examples of such thresholds include:

    * Minimum cryptographic strength (e.g. AES-128 or higher);
    * Password policy limits (e.g. minimum of 12 characters);
    * Login protection mechanisms (e.g. account lockout after five consecutive failed attempts);
    * Resistance to a specified attack potential (e.g. equivalent to CSA High/AVA_VAN.3 or higher).

  * Fail: The assessment is considered failed if the requirement is not fulfilled, or if the defined security thresholds are not achieved (e.g. insufficient key length, missing authentication enforcement, or inadequate resistance to the required attack potential).

* Assessment evidence: Defines the artefacts and documentation collected to demonstrate that the requirement has been assessed and fulfilled. The evidence shall be sufficient to enable independent verification of the assessment results and to demonstrate compliance with the relevant CRA essential requirements. The supporting evidence include, where applicable:

  * Test or assessment reports showing the steps performed and results obtained;
  * Logs, configuration files, or audit traces demonstrating the implementation of the requirement;
  * Screenshots, captures, or console outputs confirming the correct execution or protection behaviour;
  * Relevant vendor or design documentation describing the applied security measures;
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-NKEV: No known exploitable vulnerabilities

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-KEVM
TITLE: [AC-KEVM] Assessment criteria: Documentation of mitigation of known exploitable vulnerabilities
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-KEVM.

**Assessment objective**

Prevent exploitation of known exploitable vulnerabilities.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Compile a list of known exploitable vulnerabilities in the product and its components by consulting public vulnerability databases (such as CVE, NVD) and the manufacturer's own vulnerability records.

**Assessment activities**

1) Compare the generated list of known exploitable vulnerabilities with the manufacturer's documentation of remediated and outstanding vulnerabilities.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) At least one known exploitable vulnerability is neither remediated nor documented with its residual risk and any compensating control, and exceeds the manufacturer's documented vulnerability remediation timeline without documented justification.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) No known exploitable vulnerabilities were found, or every known exploitable vulnerability is remediated, documented with its residual risk and any compensating control, or within the manufacturer's documented vulnerability remediation timeline with assessment and remediation in progress.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documented vulnerability handling policy, list of known exploitable vulnerabilities, documentation of remediation or residual risk for each vulnerability, correlation of the list with the manufacturer's records.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVM

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-KEVT
TITLE: [AC-KEVT] Assessment criteria: Testing for known exploitable vulnerabilities
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-KEVT.

**Assessment objective**

Prevent exploitation of known exploitable vulnerabilities.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Compile a list of known exploitable vulnerabilities in the product and its components by consulting public vulnerability databases (such as CVE, NVD) and the manufacturer's own vulnerability records.

2) Select the vulnerabilities to be tested, prioritising by severity, known active exploitation, impact on the product, and privilege escalation potential.

3) Collect or prepare test procedures for each selected vulnerability.

**Assessment activities**

1) On a new product, run the tests and compare the results with the generated list of known exploitable vulnerabilities.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) At least one tested vulnerability is neither mitigated nor documented with the residual risk and any compensating control, and exceeds the manufacturer's documented vulnerability remediation timeline without documented justification.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) No known exploitable vulnerabilities found, or every tested vulnerability is mitigated, documented with the residual risk and any compensating control, or within the manufacturer's documented vulnerability remediation timeline with assessment and remediation in progress.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documented vulnerability handling policy, list of known exploitable vulnerabilities, test selection rationale, test results for each tested vulnerability, documentation of residual risk for any tested vulnerability that is not mitigated.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVT

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-MINI: Minimise impact on other devices and services

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-RRIS
TITLE: [AC-RRIS] Assessment criteria: Document residual risk to other devices and services
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-RRIS.

**Assessment objective**

Minimise impact on other devices and services on the network.

**Assessment activities**

1) Examine the documentation.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Residual risk to other devices and services on the network is not documented, or the documentation does not state the actions the user or the operational environment can take.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Residual risk to other devices and services on the network is documented, including the actions the user or the operational environment can take to mitigate it.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation, analysis of documentation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-RRIS

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-MNET
TITLE: [AC-MNET] Assessment criteria: Minimise negative impact of network transmission
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-MNET.

**Assessment objective**

Verify that the product implements and documents mechanisms that limit the rate and volume of the network traffic it transmits to minimise adverse impact on other products or services on the network.

**Assessment preparation**

1) The manufacturer's documentation of the mechanisms that limit the rate and volume of the network traffic the product transmits, their default configuration, and how they minimise potential interference with other products or services on the network is available.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to confirm it covers the mechanisms that limit the rate and volume of the network traffic the product transmits, details their default configuration, and explains how they minimise potential interference with other products or services on the network.

2) Inspect the product in its default state to verify that the documented rate- and volume-limiting mechanisms are present and configured as described.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) The documentation does not detail the mechanisms that limit the rate and volume of the transmitted network traffic, their default configuration, or how they minimise potential interference.

2) The product's active default configuration does not match the documented rate- and volume-limiting mechanisms.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The documentation details the mechanisms that limit the rate and volume of the transmitted network traffic, their default configuration, and how they minimise potential interference with other products or services on the network.

2) The product's active default configuration matches the documented rate- and volume-limiting mechanisms.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation of the transmit rate- and volume-limiting mechanisms, their default configuration, and the interference-minimisation rationale.

2) Test logs or inspection results verifying the active rate- and volume-limiting configuration on the product in its default state.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-MNET

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-SDEF: Secure by default configuration

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-ADEF
TITLE: [AC-ADEF] Assessment criteria: Authorisation required by default to access security-relevant assets
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-ADEF.

**Assessment objective**

Find any unauthorised access to security-relevant assets in default configuration and confirm the authentication mechanism and the authorisation rules, including any expected from the integrator, are documented.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Obtain the manufacturer's documentation of the classes of security-relevant assets. For each documented class, identify the interfaces or access paths through which the product exposes it.

**Assessment activities**

1) For each documented class of security-relevant asset, using the interfaces or access paths identified for it, attempt to access the asset without authentication and authorisation and record whether access was allowed or not.

2) Assess the documentation provided with the product and, where the product is intended for integration into another product, the documentation of security-relevant assets whose authentication and authorisation are expected to be provided by the integrator. For each class of security-relevant asset, confirm the documented authentication mechanism and authorisation rules match the behaviour observed in the access attempts.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) For at least one documented class of security-relevant asset, an interface or access path identified for it allows access without authentication and authorisation.

2) The authentication mechanism or the authorisation rules are not documented for at least one class of security-relevant asset, or do not match the observed behaviour, or any asset whose authentication and authorisation are expected from the integrator is not documented.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) For each documented class of security-relevant asset, no interface or access path identified for it allows access without authentication and authorisation.

2) The authentication mechanism and the authorisation rules are documented for each class of security-relevant asset and match the observed behaviour, and any assets whose authentication and authorisation are expected from the integrator are documented.

**Assessment evidence**

1) The manufacturer's documentation of the classes of security-relevant assets and the interfaces or access paths identified for each, the record of activities used to attempt unauthorised access, and the log of results of attempts.

2) Documentation of the authentication mechanism and authorisation rules for each class of security-relevant asset, and the comparison against the observed access-attempt results.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-ADEF

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-PDDI-1
TITLE: [AC-PDDI-1] Assessment criteria: Document how to control access to debug and management interfaces
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-PDDI-1.

**Assessment objective**

Confirm that all debug and management interfaces are documented and that the documentation specifies means to control access to or disable them.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Examine the documentation for how to control access to or disable the debug/management interfaces of the product.

**Assessment activities**

1) Examine the product for undocumented debug/management interfaces, then follow the instructions in the documentation to disable or control access to each documented interface, then attempt to access the interface without authorisation.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any debug/management interface is not documented or any interface is accessible without authorisation after following the documentation to control access to or disable them.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) All debug/management interfaces are documented as to how to disable or control access to them, and no interfaces are accessible without authorisation after following the documentation to control access to or disable them.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Pictures of the product, list of discovered interfaces, comparison with documentation, notes as to which are documented how to disable or control access to, logs of access control and disable actions, logs of attempts to access interfaces after access was controlled or they were disabled.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-PDDI-1

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-PDDI-2
TITLE: [AC-PDDI-2] Assessment criteria: Control local software access to debug and management interfaces
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-PDDI-2.

**Assessment objective**

Confirm that debug and management interfaces accessible by processes running on the system are access controlled or disabled by default.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Examine the documentation of the localhost-accessible debug and management interfaces of the product and follow the instructions to mitigate the risk of any necessary interfaces that are not access controlled or are enabled.

**Assessment activities**

1) As an unprivileged process running on the system, attempt to access the system's local debug and management interfaces and make unauthorised changes. Additionally, scan accessible memory and inter-process-communication mechanisms for undocumented debug and management interfaces.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any undocumented interface is found or any interface can be accessed without authorisation and is not documented as necessary, or, where the product allows the user to remove such protections, the instructions to the user do not document the procedure, the residual risk, or the permitted user roles.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) No undocumented interfaces are found and no interfaces can be accessed without authorisation other than those documented as necessary, and, where the product allows the user to remove such protections, the instructions to the user document the procedure, the residual risk, and the permitted user roles.

**Assessment evidence**

1) List of interfaces, log of attempts to access.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-PDDI-2

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-PDDI-3
TITLE: [AC-PDDI-3] Assessment criteria: Control network access to debug or management interfaces
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-PDDI-3.

**Assessment objective**

Confirm that debug and management interfaces accessible via the network are access controlled or disabled by default.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Examine the documentation of the network accessible debug and management interfaces of the product and follow the instructions to mitigate the risk of any necessary interfaces that are not access controlled or are enabled.

**Assessment activities**

1) Using a network scanner, scan the product for both documented and undocumented debug or remote management interfaces and determine whether they are enabled or access controlled.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any undocumented interface is found or any interface can be accessed without authorisation and is not documented as necessary, or documentation regarding the removal of these protections is provided but does not include information regarding the risks.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) No undocumented interfaces are found and no interfaces can be accessed without authorisation other than those documented as necessary, and any documentation provided regarding the removal of these protections includes information regarding the risks.

**Assessment evidence**

1) List of interfaces, log of attempts to access.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-PDDI-3

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-SCUD: Secure updates

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-KEVD
TITLE: [AC-KEVD] Assessment criteria: Documentation for secure update before or during first use
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-KEVD.

**Assessment objective**

Verify that the product documents how it may be securely updated, including before or during first use.

**Assessment preparation**

1) The manufacturer's documentation describing how the product may be securely updated, including how to update it prior to, or as part of, first use, is available.

**Assessment activities**

1) Assess the documentation to determine whether it describes how the product may be securely updated, including how to update the product prior to, or as part of, first use.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) The documentation does not describe how the product may be securely updated, or does not describe how to update the product prior to, or as part of, first use.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The documentation describes how the product may be securely updated, including how to update the product prior to, or as part of, first use.

**Assessment evidence**

1) The product documentation and analysis of its completeness.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVD

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-KEVA
TITLE: [AC-KEVA] Assessment criteria: Secure update before or during first use
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-KEVA.

**Assessment objective**

Verify that the product's secure update mechanism operates before or during first use, that an authorised user can opt out of automatic security updates, and that the user is informed to initiate or postpone the update after opting out.

**Assessment preparation**

1) The manufacturer's instructions for first-time installation and use of the product, and the secure updates the manufacturer makes available for the product, are available.

**Assessment activities**

1) Follow the instructions to install and use the product for the first time, and verify that the secure update mechanism operates by default before or during first use and applies the secure updates the manufacturer makes available.

2) Locate and exercise the product's mechanism for opting out of automatic security updates, which should be available both at first use and during subsequent use.

3) Opt out of automatic security updates using that mechanism, then follow the instructions to install and use the product for the first time.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) The secure update mechanism does not operate by default before or during first use, or does not apply the available secure updates.

2) The product provides no mechanism allowing an authorised user to opt out of automatic security updates, or the mechanism is not clear and easy to use.

3) After an authorised user opts out of automatic security updates, the product does not provide, before or during first use, a mechanism to inform the user to initiate or postpone the update.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The secure update mechanism operates by default before or during first use and applies the available secure updates.

2) The product provides a clear and easy-to-use mechanism allowing an authorised user to opt out of automatic security updates.

3) After an authorised user opts out of automatic security updates, the product provides, before or during first use, a mechanism to inform the user to initiate or postpone the update.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Record of the first-use update behaviour and the updates applied.

2) The mechanism for opting out of automatic security updates and its documentation.

3) Record of the first-use behaviour after opting out, showing the mechanism to inform the user to initiate or postpone the update.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-KEVA

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-SCUE
TITLE: [AC-SCUE] Assessment criteria: Security updates provided by the operational environment
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-SCUE.

**Assessment objective**

Confirm that the product can receive and apply secure updates provided by the operational environment, and that the manufacturer documents the requirements the operational environment shall satisfy to deliver them.

**Assessment activities**

1) Assess the documentation provided with the product.

2) Through the operational-environment update path documented by the manufacturer, provide a secure update whose authenticity and integrity can be verified, and confirm the product receives and applies it; then provide an update whose authenticity or integrity cannot be verified, and confirm the product rejects it in accordance with MI-SUVI.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not describe requirements for the secure updates provided by the operational environment.

2) The product does not receive and apply the update whose authenticity and integrity are verified, or accepts an update whose authenticity or integrity cannot be verified.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation describes requirements for the secure updates provided by the operational environment.

2) The product receives and applies the update whose authenticity and integrity are verified, and rejects the update whose authenticity or integrity cannot be verified.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation and analysis of completeness.

2) Record of the updates provided through the operational-environment update path and the product's acceptance or rejection of each.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-SCUE

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-SUVI
TITLE: [AC-SUVI] Assessment criteria: Update authenticity and integrity verification
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-SUVI.

**Assessment objective**

Verify that the product rejects update packages whose authenticity or integrity cannot be verified before installation.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Identify, from the documentation, each update path of the product and the mechanism by which the authenticity and integrity of update packages are verified for that path.

**Assessment activities**

1) For each update path, identify the verification mechanism it uses, and attempt to deliver an update that is not authentic or not integral by that mechanism, for example a tampered payload, an unsigned or wrongly-signed package, delivery from an unauthorised source, or delivery over an unauthenticated channel, and observe whether the product installs it. Additionally attempt to bypass or disable the verification for that update path, for example through a debug flag, an API override, a boot parameter, or an unmanaged update path, and observe whether the verification remains enforced.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) For any update path, an update that is not authentic or not integral is installed, or the verification can be bypassed without user consent.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) For every update path, an update that is not authentic or not integral is not installed, by whichever verification mechanism applies to that update path, and the verification cannot be bypassed without user consent.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation of the update paths and their verification mechanisms, and test results showing that updates which are not authentic or not integral are not installed and that the verification cannot be bypassed without user consent.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-SUVI

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-SUDP
TITLE: [AC-SUDP] Assessment criteria: Anti-rollback protection
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-SUDP.

**Assessment objective**

Verify that the product prevents the unauthorised installation of an earlier software version.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Identify the current software version of the product and obtain an authorised update package for an earlier version.

**Assessment activities**

1) Attempt to install the earlier version on the product without authorisation and observe whether the product installs it.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) The product installs the earlier version without authorisation.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The product prevents unauthorised installation of the earlier version.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Test results showing the product prevents the unauthorised installation of an earlier version.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-SUDP

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-SUAR
TITLE: [AC-SUAR] Assessment criteria: Authorised rollback
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-SUAR.

**Assessment objective**

Verify that intentional, user-initiated rollback to an earlier software version requires explicit authorisation, and that automated recovery mechanisms are treated as recovery actions exempt from that requirement.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Confirm from the documentation whether the product supports intentional, user-initiated rollback and identify the authorisation required.

2) Identify any automated recovery mechanisms that revert to the immediately preceding operational state following a failed update or boot failure.

**Assessment activities**

1) Attempt to perform an intentional, user-initiated rollback to an earlier version without the required authorisation, then repeat with the required authorisation.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) The product performs an intentional, user-initiated rollback without the required authorisation.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The product performs an intentional, user-initiated rollback only when the required authorisation is provided.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Test results showing intentional, user-initiated rollback is performed only with the required authorisation, and documentation distinguishing automated recovery from rollback.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-SUAR

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-AUTH: Authentication and access control

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-RAUT
TITLE: [AC-RAUT] Assessment criteria: Authenticate the remote data processing solution and validate its interactions at the RDPS boundary
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-RAUT.

**Assessment objective**

Authenticate the remote data processing solution and verify the authenticity, integrity, and freshness of interactions received across the RDPS boundary.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Documentation describing the authentication mechanism used for communication with each remote data processing solution is available.

2) Documentation describing the mechanisms used to verify the authenticity and integrity of received interactions and to detect replayed or stale interactions is available.

3) The product is in operational state with remote data processing solution connectivity configured.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to identify the authentication mechanism used by the product to authenticate the remote data processing solution before transmitting data or acting upon received interactions.

2) Substitute the remote data processing solution with an endpoint that does not satisfy authentication. Observe whether the product transmits data to, or acts upon interactions from, the substituted endpoint.

3) Present to the product an interaction that carries the identity of the authenticated remote data processing solution but whose content has been modified after it was produced. Observe whether the product acts upon the modified interaction.

4) Replay to the product an interaction that was previously valid, in a context where replay or reuse could affect the secure operation of a product function. Observe whether the product acts upon the replayed interaction.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not describe the authentication mechanism used by the product to authenticate the remote data processing solution.

2) The product transmits data to, or acts upon interactions from, an endpoint that does not satisfy authentication.

3) The product acts upon the modified interaction.

4) The product acts upon the replayed or stale interaction.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation describes the authentication mechanism used by the product to authenticate the remote data processing solution.

2) The product does not transmit data to, or act upon interactions from, an endpoint that does not satisfy authentication.

3) The product rejects the modified interaction and does not act upon it.

4) The product detects and rejects the replayed or stale interaction.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation describing the authentication mechanism used by the product.

2) Test results showing the product authenticates the remote data processing solution before transmitting data or acting upon interactions.

3) Test results showing the product rejects interactions whose integrity is not intact.

4) Test results showing the product detects and rejects replayed or stale interactions.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-RAUT

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-AUTH-1
TITLE: [AC-AUTH-1] Assessment criteria: Require user authentication on interfaces providing access to the operating system
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-AUTH-1.

**Assessment objective**

Require user authentication by default on interfaces providing access to the operating system.

**Assessment preparation**

1) List all interfaces through which the operating system exposes access to operating system resources or functions to users, including login interfaces (such as PAM-based text and graphical logins, remote shell daemons, console interfaces) and programmatic interfaces requiring user context.

2) Documentation describing the user authentication mechanism applied to each interface by default, the set of functions the interface exposes, and any interfaces deliberately not requiring user authentication by default together with the justification for the exception.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that every interface providing user access to operating system resources or functions either requires user authentication by default or is documented as deliberately unauthenticated with justification.

2) For each documented authenticated interface, attempt to access operating system resources or functions through that interface without presenting valid user authentication. Verify that access is denied.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any interface providing user access neither requires user authentication by default nor is documented as deliberately unauthenticated with justification.

2) Any documented authenticated interface grants access without valid user authentication.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Every interface either requires user authentication by default or is documented as deliberately unauthenticated with justification.

2) Every documented authenticated interface denies access without valid user authentication.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation listing every interface, its user authentication mechanism, and any unauthenticated-interface justifications.

2) Test results showing that each authenticated interface denies access without user authentication.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-AUTH-1

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-AUTH-2
TITLE: [AC-AUTH-2] Assessment criteria: Require privilege or authorisation on interfaces providing access to the operating system
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-AUTH-2.

**Assessment objective**

Ensure that interfaces providing access to operating system resources or functions not intended for unauthenticated use require the account to hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation.

**Assessment preparation**

1) List all interfaces through which the operating system exposes access to operating system resources or functions not intended for unauthenticated use, including administrative login interfaces, privilege-elevation utilities, management APIs, administrative tooling, and remote management interfaces provided by the operating system.

2) Documentation describing the privilege or authorisation required to invoke each interface, the set of functions the interface exposes, and any interfaces deliberately not requiring privilege or authorisation together with the justification for the exception.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that every interface providing access to operating system resources or functions not intended for unauthenticated use either requires the account to hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation or is documented as deliberately unauthenticated with justification.

2) For each documented interface, attempt to invoke it as an unauthorised account, without holding the appropriate privilege or authorisation. Verify that access is denied.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any interface providing access to operating system resources or functions not intended for unauthenticated use neither requires the appropriate privilege or authorisation nor is documented as deliberately unauthenticated with justification.

2) Any documented interface grants access to an unauthorised account that does not hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Every interface either requires the account to hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation or is documented as deliberately unauthenticated with justification.

2) Every documented interface denies access to an unauthorised account that does not hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation listing every such interface, the privilege or authorisation it requires, and any unauthenticated-interface justifications.

2) Test results showing that each interface denies access to an unauthorised account that does not hold the appropriate privilege or authorisation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-AUTH-2

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-LCKT
TITLE: [AC-LCKT] Assessment criteria: Authentication failure protection
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-LCKT.

**Assessment objective**

Prevent brute-force attacks against operating system authentication interfaces.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Documentation describing, for each authentication interface, the authentication failure protection that the operating system enforces by default.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that every authentication interface is associated with a documented authentication failure protection mechanism.

2) For each authentication interface, with the product in its default configuration, perform repeated authentication attempts with invalid credentials, and verify that the interface enforces the documented authentication failure protection.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any authentication interface has no documented authentication failure protection mechanism.

2) Any authentication interface does not enforce the documented authentication failure protection by default under repeated invalid authentication attempts.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Every authentication interface is associated with a documented authentication failure protection mechanism.

2) Every authentication interface enforces the documented authentication failure protection by default under repeated invalid authentication attempts.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation of the authentication failure protection enforced per authentication interface.

2) Test results showing authentication failure protection is enforced per interface.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-LCKT

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-CRED
TITLE: [AC-CRED] Assessment criteria: Protect stored critical security parameters and enforce credential strength
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-CRED.

**Assessment objective**

Protect stored credentials and critical security parameters and enforce minimum credential strength on accepted credentials.

**Assessment preparation**

1) List all categories of critical security parameters stored by the operating system, including credential hashes or equivalent verification material, cryptographic keys managed by kernel keyrings or operating system key stores, certificate private keys, and trust anchors.

2) Documentation describing the protection mechanism applied to each category of stored critical security parameter, conforming to TR-CRYP, and the minimum credential strength requirements enforced by default by each authentication interface.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that every category of stored critical security parameter is associated with a documented protection mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP, and that every authentication interface is associated with a documented minimum credential strength requirement.

2) Inspect the storage representation of each category of critical security parameter on the operating system and verify that the documented protection mechanism is applied.

3) For each authentication interface, with the product in its default configuration, attempt to configure a credential that does not meet the documented minimum strength requirement. Verify that the operating system rejects the attempt.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any category of stored critical security parameter lacks a documented protection mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP, or any authentication interface lacks a documented minimum credential strength requirement.

2) The documented protection mechanism is not applied to at least one category of stored critical security parameter.

3) Any authentication interface accepts, by default, a credential that does not meet the documented minimum strength requirement.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Every category of stored critical security parameter is associated with a documented protection mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP, and every authentication interface is associated with a documented minimum credential strength requirement.

2) The documented protection mechanism is applied to every category of stored critical security parameter.

3) Every authentication interface rejects, by default, credentials that do not meet the documented minimum strength requirement.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation listing categories of stored critical security parameters with their protection mechanisms, and the minimum credential strength requirements per authentication interface.

2) Test results showing the storage representation of each category of critical security parameter and the protection mechanism applied.

3) Test results showing rejection of low-strength credentials per authentication interface.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-CRED

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-ACCS
TITLE: [AC-ACCS] Assessment criteria: Access control enforcement on operating system resources and functions
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-ACCS.

**Assessment objective**

Enforce access control on operating system resources and functions.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Documentation describing the access control model used by the operating system, including the mechanism (such as discretionary access control through filesystem permissions, POSIX capabilities, mandatory access control frameworks, role-based access control, or compile-time fixed access rules), the protected resource categories, and the authorisation attributes considered when granting access.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that the access control model is described, the protected resource categories are enumerated, and the authorisation attributes are defined.

2) For each protected resource category, attempt access from a subject that is authorised according to the documented model and verify the access succeeds. Attempt access from a subject that is not authorised according to the documented model and verify the access is denied.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not describe the access control model, the protected resource categories, or the authorisation attributes.

2) Any authorised subject is denied access or any unauthorised subject is permitted access for any protected resource category.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation describes the access control model, the protected resource categories, and the authorisation attributes.

2) Authorised subjects are permitted access and unauthorised subjects are denied access for every protected resource category.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation describing the access control model, protected resource categories, and authorisation attributes.

2) Test results showing access outcomes for authorised and unauthorised subjects per protected resource category.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-ACCS

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-PRIV
TITLE: [AC-PRIV] Assessment criteria: Privilege separation and restriction
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-PRIV.

**Assessment objective**

Enforce privilege separation, restrict subjects to their assigned privilege level, and deny in-session privilege escalation without re-authentication or other documented authorisation, on operating systems supporting multiple privilege levels.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Documentation describing the privilege-level model of the operating system, including the distinct privilege levels (such as kernel and user mode, POSIX effective user identifiers, POSIX capabilities sets, or ring-based privilege hierarchies), the operations permitted at each privilege level, the transitions between privilege levels, and the authorisation required for privilege escalation within an active session.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that the distinct privilege levels, the operations permitted at each privilege level, the transitions between privilege levels, and the authorisation required for privilege escalation are described.

2) For each privilege level, attempt to perform operations not permitted at that privilege level from a subject executing at that privilege level. Verify that the operating system denies the operation.

3) In an active session, attempt to escalate to a higher privilege level without providing the documented authorisation, and verify that the escalation is denied.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not describe the distinct privilege levels, the operations permitted at each privilege level, the transitions between privilege levels, or the authorisation required for privilege escalation.

2) Any subject can perform an operation not permitted at its assigned privilege level.

3) Privilege escalation within an active session succeeds without the documented authorisation.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation describes the distinct privilege levels, the operations permitted at each privilege level, the transitions between privilege levels, and the authorisation required for privilege escalation.

2) Every subject is restricted to the operations permitted at its assigned privilege level.

3) Privilege escalation within an active session is denied without the documented authorisation.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation describing the privilege-level model.

2) Test results showing denial of unauthorised operations per privilege level.

3) Test results showing that in-session privilege escalation is denied without the documented authorisation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-PRIV

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-SESS
TITLE: [AC-SESS] Assessment criteria: Interactive session lifecycle controls
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-SESS.

**Assessment objective**

Enforce lifecycle controls on authenticated interactive sessions.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Documentation describing the interactive session model of the operating system, including the events that trigger session invalidation, and describing any sessions or security tokens derived from the interactive session that remain valid beyond the termination event.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that the interactive session model and the session-invalidation triggers are described.

2) Establish an authenticated interactive session, perform session logout or termination, and verify that the interactive session is invalidated upon logout or termination according to the product's documented session model.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not describe the interactive session model or the session-invalidation triggers.

2) The interactive session is not invalidated upon logout or termination according to the product's documented session model.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation describes the interactive session model and the session-invalidation triggers.

2) The interactive session is invalidated upon logout or termination according to the product's documented session model.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation describing the interactive session lifecycle.

2) Test results showing session invalidation upon logout or termination according to the product's documented session model.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-SESS

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-ACSD
TITLE: [AC-ACSD] Assessment criteria: Access control for stored data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-ACSD.

**Assessment objective**

Protect stored data from unauthorised access and modification.

**Assessment preparation**

1) List the types of data stored on the product that are protected from unauthorised access or modification, and for each the mechanism the manufacturer documents, whether access controls and privilege separation at the operating system level, another documented mechanism, or a documented formal proof, and the methods of accessing or modifying that data available to an attacker based on the risk assessment.

**Assessment activities**

1) For each type of stored data and each access method, determine the mechanism used to prevent unauthorised access and attempt to read the data without authorisation.

2) For each type of stored data, where the mechanism is enforced at runtime, attempt to modify the data without authorisation.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) At least one attempt to read the stored data without authorisation succeeds, or the mechanism preventing unauthorised access for a type of stored data is not documented.

2) At least one attempt to modify the stored data without authorisation succeeds, or the mechanism preventing unauthorised modification for a type of stored data is not documented.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The product prevents unauthorised access to the stored data, whether by access controls and privilege separation, by another documented mechanism, or by a documented formal proof, and all attempts to read the data without authorisation fail.

2) The product prevents unauthorised modification of the stored data, whether by access controls and privilege separation, by another documented mechanism, or by a documented formal proof, and all attempts to modify the data without authorisation fail.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation of the access-protection mechanism per data type, and results of attempts to read the data without authorisation.

2) Documentation of the modification-protection mechanism per data type, and results of attempts to modify the data without authorisation.

.. note::
  NOTE: Guidance: Stored data may be protected from unauthorised access or modification by the environment, permissions, encryption, salting and hashing, offline storage, hardware-backed secrets, duplication, backups, or checksums; where cryptographic mechanisms are used, the requirements of TR-CRYP apply. A formal proof, in a formal verification tool, that the product protects stored data from unauthorised access or modification is an acceptable alternative means of demonstrating conformance with this mitigation, provided the tool selection and configuration criteria, the formal definition of confidentiality and integrity, and the formal argument applying it to a formalisation of the product's source code are documented.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-ACSD

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-CDST: Confidentiality of data stored on the product

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-ENST
TITLE: [AC-ENST] Assessment criteria: Cryptographic encryption of stored data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-ENST.

**Assessment objective**

Verify the product provides, by default, the option to protect the confidentiality of stored user data at rest using a cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Obtain manufacturer documentation describing the cryptographic encryption mechanism the product provides by default as an option for stored user data, including the algorithm used, the keying material or key-derivation mechanism, and the hardware-backed or software-backed key-storage mechanism used to protect the encryption keys.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that the product provides, by default, an option to protect the confidentiality of stored user data at rest using a cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

2) Starting from the product in its default configuration, enable the documented encryption option and inspect the operating system in its operational state, examining the raw storage representation of the stored user data. Verify that, when the option is enabled, the documented cryptographic encryption mechanism is applied and that the algorithm in use matches the documentation and conforms to TR-CRYP.

3) With the encryption option enabled, attempt to recover the stored content from the raw storage representation without access to the keying material. Verify that the content cannot be recovered.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not describe an option available in the product's default configuration to protect stored user data at rest using a cryptographic encryption mechanism, or the documented mechanism does not conform to TR-CRYP.

2) The encryption option is not available in the default configuration or cannot be enabled, or when enabled the cryptographic encryption mechanism is not applied or the algorithm in use does not conform to TR-CRYP.

3) When the encryption option is enabled, the stored content can be recovered from the raw storage representation without access to the keying material.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation describes an option, available in the product's default configuration, to protect stored user data at rest using a cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

2) The encryption option is available in the default configuration and can be enabled and, when enabled, the cryptographic encryption mechanism is applied to the stored user data and the algorithm in use conforms to TR-CRYP.

3) When the encryption option is enabled, the stored content cannot be recovered from the raw storage representation without access to the keying material.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation describing the option to encrypt stored user data and the cryptographic encryption mechanism it uses.

2) Test results showing the raw storage representation of stored user data with the encryption option enabled and the cryptographic encryption mechanism applied to it.

3) Test results showing that, with the encryption option enabled, stored content cannot be recovered from the raw storage representation without access to the keying material.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-ENST

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-CDTX: Confidentiality of data transmitted by the product

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-CDTX
TITLE: [AC-CDTX] Assessment criteria: Protect confidentiality of user data transmitted by the product
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-CDTX.

**Assessment objective**

Protect, by default, user data transmitted by the product from unauthorised access.

**Assessment preparation**

1) List all types of data that may be transmitted on the product that should not be readable without authorisation, the confidentiality mechanism documented by the manufacturer as applied by default for each type, all methods of accessing that data available to an attacker based on the risk assessment, and the authorisation methods documented by the manufacturer for each access method.

**Assessment activities**

1) With the product in its default configuration, for each type of data and each access mechanism, determine the method of ensuring confidentiality used, and attempt to read the data without authorisation.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Any method of ensuring confidentiality applied by default does not match the type of the data transmitted, or any attempt to read confidential data without authorisation succeeds.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) All methods of ensuring confidentiality applied by default match the type of the data transmitted, and all the attempts to read confidential data without authorisation fail.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Logs of determination of type of data and method of confidentiality and attempts to read confidential data without authorisation.

.. note::
  NOTE: Guidance: Data transmitted may be protected by the environment or by encryption. Where encryption is used, the requirements of TR-CRYP apply.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-CDTX

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-RRDC
TITLE: [AC-RRDC] Assessment criteria: Document residual risk to confidentiality of data transmitted
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-RRDC.

**Assessment objective**

Confirm that the product is accompanied by documentation describing the residual risk to the confidentiality of transmitted user data and the actions that can be taken to mitigate it.

**Assessment activities**

1) Examine the documentation.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Transfer of risk is not documented, or the documentation does not state the residual risks or the user mitigations.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Transfer of risk is documented, including the residual risks accepted by the user and the actions the user can take to mitigate them.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation, analysis of documentation.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-RRDC

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-ENTX
TITLE: [AC-ENTX] Assessment criteria: Cryptographic encryption of transmitted data
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-ENTX.

**Assessment objective**

Protect the confidentiality of transmitted user data, by default, using a cryptographic encryption mechanism.

**Assessment preparation**

1) List all categories of user data transmitted by the operating system, including data transmitted through operating-system-provided network protocols (such as TLS, SSH, IPsec, and VPN client or server implementations), remote administration interfaces, remote filesystem and file sharing protocols, remote logging and audit streams, remote management or telemetry channels, and channels exchanging credentials, cryptographic keys, or other critical security parameters.

2) Documentation describing the cryptographic encryption mechanism applied by default to each category of transmitted user data is available, including the algorithm used, the keying material or session key used, and the key establishment or negotiation mechanism.

**Assessment activities**

1) Review the documentation to verify that every category of transmitted user data is associated with a documented cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

2) Inspect the operating system in its default configuration while it transmits data in each documented category. Capture the transmitted data on the network and verify that the documented cryptographic encryption mechanism is applied and that the algorithm in use matches the documentation and conforms to TR-CRYP.

3) For each category of transmitted user data, attempt to recover the transmitted content from a passive network capture without access to the keying material. Verify that the content cannot be recovered.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) Documentation does not associate every category of transmitted user data with a cryptographic encryption mechanism, or any documented mechanism does not conform to TR-CRYP.

2) The cryptographic encryption mechanism is not applied by default to at least one category of transmitted user data, or the algorithm in use does not conform to TR-CRYP.

3) The transmitted content can be recovered from the network capture without access to the keying material.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) Documentation associates every category of transmitted user data with a cryptographic encryption mechanism conforming to TR-CRYP.

2) The cryptographic encryption mechanism is applied by default to every category of transmitted user data and the algorithm in use conforms to TR-CRYP.

3) The transmitted content cannot be recovered from the network capture without access to the keying material.

**Assessment evidence**

1) Documentation listing every category of transmitted user data and the cryptographic encryption mechanism applied to each.

2) Test results from network traffic captures showing the cryptographic encryption mechanism applied to each category of transmitted user data and the algorithm in use.

3) Test results showing that transmitted content cannot be recovered from the network capture without access to the keying material.
<<<
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: ETSI-OS-MI-ENTX

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: TR-CRYP: Encryption

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: ETSI-OS-AC-CRYP
TITLE: [AC-CRYP] Assessment criteria: Conformance to Annex K
DESCRIPTION: >>>
**Assessment reference**

Mitigation MI-CRYP.

**Assessment objective**

Confirm that the product conforms to Annex K.

**Assessment preparation**

1) Documentation describing the cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration, as required by Annex K.

**Assessment activities**

1) Assess the product against Annex K, applying the assessment of the product's cryptographic configuration in clause K.1.2 and the assessment of crypto-agility in clause K.2.2.

**Assessment verdict**

The verdict fail is assigned if any of the following conditions apply:

1) The product does not conform to Annex K.

The verdict pass is assigned if none of the above conditions apply and all of the following conditions are met:

1) The product conforms to Annex K.

**Assessment evidence**

1) The evidence required by the assessments in Annex K.
<<<
