<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TH-SUPP
LEVEL: B.4.16
TITLE: TH-SUPP: Supply chain compromise

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
An attacker compromises a dependency used in the product's build process, such as a low-level driver, cryptographic library, or firmware, to inject a backdoor or exploitable vulnerability.

Assets affected: Software assets, Data assets, Identity and access assets, and Product functions.

The product's build process is outside the visibility and control of product-based protection mechanisms, so attacks on its integrity remain a residual risk that cannot be fully mitigated by the product.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
UID: ETSI-OS-TH-HARD
LEVEL: B.4.17
TITLE: TH-HARD: Compromise via hardware-level or platform trust components

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Hardware-level capabilities, for example a compromised CPU, firmware, hardware security module, or physical access to deployed systems, can compromise the product or associated platform components below the software control plane. Such attacks can exploit manufacturer backdoors in processors or firmware, supply chain implants in platform components, or physical tampering vectors.

Assets affected: Software assets, Data assets, Identity and access assets, and Product functions.

Because hardware-level compromise can operate at privilege levels that exceed the visibility and control of software-based protection mechanisms, these attacks can bypass or undermine the integrity verification, integrity checks, and isolation mechanisms defined in the present document. Hardware-level compromise remains a residual risk that cannot be fully mitigated by the product.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.5
TITLE: Mapping of use cases to risk factors

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.5.1
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Each basic use case in clause 4.7.2 is associated with a baseline level for every risk factor defined in clause B.2. The basic use case is determined by a single capability of the product, namely whether it is a restricted operating system (UC-REST) or a general-purpose computing platform (UC-GEN). Each condition in clause 4.7.3 raises one or more risk factors. The risk profile of a product is obtained by taking, for each risk factor, the highest level assigned by the product's basic use case and by any condition that holds for it, as described in clause 4.7.4. Where the product is characterised as UC-GEN and a condition cannot be determined, the condition is taken to hold. This clause records the baseline levels and the condition increments from which the security profiles in clause B.6 are derived. Levels follow clause B.2, where level 0 is the lowest and level 2 the highest.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.5.2
TITLE: Baseline risk-factor levels of the basic use cases

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Table B.5-1 gives the baseline level that each basic use case assigns to each risk factor. UC-REST begins from the lowest levels for all capability and exposure factors, reflecting a restricted, self-administered product; UC-GEN begins from the levels of a minimal single-user general-purpose system. All further capabilities and exposures are added through the conditions in clause B.5.3.

.. list-table:: Table B.5-1: Baseline risk-factor levels of the basic use cases
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 60 20 20

   * - Risk factor
     - UC-REST
     - UC-GEN
   * - RF-NUSR Number of user accounts
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-CUSR User-account concurrency
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-PPII Collection of PII
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SNDS Sensitivity of data stored
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SNDT Sensitivity of data transmitted
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SENF Sensitivity of functions
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-PHYS Physical access by threat actors
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-UEIN Untrusted external inputs
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-LOSS Probability of loss/theft
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-HWMD Hardware modifiability
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SWMD Software modifiability
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-DVCS Untrusted peripheral devices
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-TNET Access to a public network
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-FNET Reached from untrusted networks
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-CONF Configurability
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-ADMN Administration
     - 2
     - 1
   * - RF-SUPP Support and foreseeable updates
     - 1
     - 2
   * - RF-RDPS Remote data-processing dependency
     - 0
     - 0

.. note::
  NOTE: Risk factors that depend on a capability or exposure the product does or does not have (network access, multiple accounts, installation of software, storage of sensitive data, physical exposure, loss likelihood, and remote data-processing dependency) are not set by the basic use case. They are set by the conditions in clause B.5.3, so that a product is rated for the capabilities and exposures it actually has.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.5.3
TITLE: Condition increments

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Table B.5-2 gives, for each condition, the risk factors it raises and the minimum level to which it raises them. A condition takes effect only where it holds for the product, as determined in clause 4.7.4. These increments mirror the conditions defined in clause 4.7.3.

.. list-table:: Table B.5-2: Risk-factor increments of the conditions
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 30 70

   * - Condition
     - Risk factors raised to at least
   * - C-NET
     - TNET-2, FNET-2, UEIN-2
   * - C-NETR
     - TNET-1
   * - C-MOD
     - SWMD-1, HWMD-1, CONF-2
   * - C-APPS
     - SWMD-2
   * - C-LOSS
     - LOSS-2, PHYS-1
   * - C-MULTI
     - NUSR-2
   * - C-UNTR
     - CUSR-2
   * - C-DATA
     - SNDS-1, SNDT-1, PPII-1
   * - C-FUNC
     - SENF-2
   * - C-RDPS
     - RDPS-1
   * - C-PHYS
     - PHYS-2, DVCS-2
   * - C-SELFADM
     - ADMN-2
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.5.4
TITLE: Deriving the risk profile of a product

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The risk profile of a product is the vector of risk-factor levels obtained by combining the baseline of its basic use case (Table B.5-1) with the increments of every condition that holds (Table B.5-2), taking the highest level for each risk factor. The risk profile is the basis for the security profile in clause B.6.

EXAMPLE: A personal laptop that connects to public networks, permits arbitrary software installation, is a foreseeable target of theft, and holds sensitive personal data is characterised as UC-GEN with conditions C-NET, C-MOD, C-LOSS, and C-DATA. Starting from the UC-GEN baseline of Table B.5-1, C-NET raises TNET, FNET, and UEIN to at least 2; C-MOD raises SWMD and HWMD to at least 1 and CONF to 2; C-LOSS raises LOSS to 2 and PHYS to 1; and C-DATA raises SNDS, SNDT, and PPII to 1. The highest level is taken for each factor, giving the product's risk profile.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.6
TITLE: Security profiles

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.6.1
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
There are two security profiles, SP-REST and SP-GEN, one for each basic use case in clause 4.7.2. The security profiles are defined normatively in clause 5.3. Each security profile is associated with the baseline risk-factor levels of its basic use case, as given in Table B.5-1. A product is assigned the security profile that corresponds to its basic use case.

The conditions in clause 4.7.3 are not separate security profiles. Each mitigation that addresses a risk introduced by a condition carries that condition as its applicability condition, as described in clause 4.7.3, and applies to a product, in addition to its security profile, wherever the condition holds, independently of the basic use case. The mitigations a product implements are therefore:

* every unconditioned mitigation listed for the product's security profile in clause 5.3; together with

* every mitigation that carries a condition the product meets, whatever the product's security profile.

.. note::
  NOTE: Expressing capabilities and exposures as a condition overlay rather than as separate profiles keeps the number of security profiles at two, instead of requiring a distinct profile for every combination of capabilities. A product's risk profile (clause B.5.4) is covered by its security profile together with the conditioned mitigations that apply to it, so no further profile selection is needed. A general-purpose product whose conditions are all assumed to hold (clause 4.7.4) implements every conditioned mitigation, which corresponds to the most demanding profile.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.6.2
TITLE: Mapping of security profiles to risk factors

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Table B.6-1 associates each security profile with the risk-factor levels of its basic use case. These are the baseline levels of Table B.5-1; the increments of Table B.5-2 are realised through the applicability of the conditioned mitigations rather than through additional security profiles.

.. list-table:: Table B.6-1: Mapping of security profiles to risk factors
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 60 20 20

   * - Risk factor
     - SP-REST
     - SP-GEN
   * - RF-NUSR Number of user accounts
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-CUSR User-account concurrency
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-PPII Collection of PII
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SNDS Sensitivity of data stored
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SNDT Sensitivity of data transmitted
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SENF Sensitivity of functions
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-PHYS Physical access by threat actors
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-UEIN Untrusted external inputs
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-LOSS Probability of loss/theft
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-HWMD Hardware modifiability
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-SWMD Software modifiability
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-DVCS Untrusted peripheral devices
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-TNET Access to a public network
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-FNET Reached from untrusted networks
     - 0
     - 0
   * - RF-CONF Configurability
     - 0
     - 1
   * - RF-ADMN Administration
     - 2
     - 1
   * - RF-SUPP Support and foreseeable updates
     - 1
     - 2
   * - RF-RDPS Remote data-processing dependency
     - 0
     - 0
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.6.3
TITLE: Determining the applicable mitigations

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The mitigations a product implements are determined as follows:

1) characterise the product as in clause 4.7.4, giving its basic use case and the conditions that hold;

2) select the security profile that corresponds to the basic use case, namely SP-REST or SP-GEN, and read its mitigations from clause 5.3;

3) implement every mitigation of that security profile that does not carry a condition;

4) implement every mitigation, of that security profile or otherwise, that carries a condition the product meets; and

5) for each mitigation whose condition does not hold, record the basis for the determination, as required by clause 4.7.4.

Where a security profile lists alternative mitigations, the manufacturer implements at least one of the listed alternatives, as specified in clause 5.3.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: B.6.4
TITLE: Relationship to the threat analysis

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Mitigations of a security profile address one or more of the threats in clause B.4 whose likelihood or impact is raised by the risk factors at the levels of that profile (Table B.6-1), and each conditioned mitigation addresses a threat whose likelihood or impact is raised by the risk factors that the corresponding condition raises (Table B.5-2). The security analysis in clause B.4 is therefore the principal basis both for the contents of the security profiles and for the conditions carried by the individual mitigations.

Two further bases apply to the baseline mitigations. First, state of the art cryptography (Annex K) and the secure update mitigations are horizontal requirements of the CRA: they apply to every product that uses cryptography or that receives updates, independently of its risk-factor levels, and are therefore included in both baselines rather than derived from a single threat tier. Second, the general-purpose baseline (SP-GEN) additionally includes the stored-data protection mitigations and the secure-development, isolation, and hardening mitigations, because a general-purpose operating system stores and processes user data and is expected to meet at least one high-exposure condition in foreseeable use. A restricted operating system reaches these same mitigations only through the conditions that apply to it.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: None
TITLE: Annexes C to J:

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Void
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: None
TITLE: Annex K (normative): Generic requirements and assessment criteria for the use of state of the art cryptography

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.0
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
This annex specifies the cryptographic requirement for the present document, the assessment of the product's cryptographic configuration, the crypto-agility requirement, and the cryptographic mechanisms recognised by the present document in addition to the European Cybersecurity Certification Group's Agreed Cryptographic Mechanisms catalogue (ACM) [1]. The terms cryptographic mechanism and cryptographic property used in this annex are defined in clause 3.1.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1
TITLE: Cryptography

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.1
TITLE: Requirement

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The product's default configuration shall only use cryptographic mechanisms that meet at least one of the following criteria:

1) ACM-listed: the cryptographic mechanism is listed in the ACM catalogue [1];

2) ACM-extended: the cryptographic mechanism is not listed in the ACM catalogue [1] and meets at least one of the following conditions:

   a) the cryptographic mechanism is listed in clause K.3.2 as an ACM-extended cryptographic mechanism for the specific product function(s);

   b) where the cryptographic mechanism is not listed in clause K.3.2, the cryptographic mechanism meets all the following criteria:

      i) the cryptographic mechanism, or where applicable the ACM-listed cryptographic mechanism on which it is based, is not deprecated per the ACM catalogue [1];

      ii) the cryptographic mechanism has been specified, developed or maintained through a transparent process by a recognised European, international or sector-specific standards development organisation, or by an industry specification organisation accountable for the relevant specification (for example ETSI, ISO/IEC, IETF, IEEE, 3GPP or ITU-T); or the cryptographic mechanism is listed as suitable in a publicly available cryptographic catalogue maintained by a recognised national or governmental cybersecurity authority, where the catalogue is maintained under a documented revision and retirement process (for example BSI TR-02102 [2], ANSSI Référentiel Général de Sécurité Annex B2 [i.14], the NIST Special Publication 800 series [4], or CCCS ITSP.40.062 [5]);

      iii) the cryptographic mechanism is described in a valid, publicly available and uniquely referenceable specification;

      iv) the cryptographic properties of the cryptographic mechanism are known;

      v) no known weakness affects the cryptographic mechanism in a way that affects its cryptographic properties;

      vi) the cryptographic mechanism is required for a specific set of product functions.

   c) interoperability-based: the cryptographic mechanism is listed in clause K.4.2 as an interoperability-based cryptographic mechanism for specific product function(s) and external specification(s) or external requirement(s).

.. note::
  NOTE 1: The reference to the product's default configuration is intended to define a clear and assessable baseline, corresponding to the configuration in which the product is placed on the market. The product can provide several configurations that fulfil the requirement.

.. note::
  NOTE 2: For products supplied as hardware platforms or components to be configured by an integrator, references in this annex to the product's default configuration refer to the configuration specified for the intended operational use of the product after integration, including the relevant integration assumptions and configuration constraints.

.. note::
  NOTE 3: The applicable ACM catalogue version is identified in the normative references of the present document. The lifecycle treatment of mechanisms affected by ACM deprecation dates, expiry dates, migration conditions or usage limitations is addressed in clause K.2.

.. note::
  NOTE 4: In this clause, an external specification or external requirement means a specification or requirement that is imposed on the product, and which requires the use of a specific cryptographic mechanism for the product to interoperate with an identified system, platform or operational context. An external requirement can be a regulatory requirement, operational constraint, technical interoperability constraint, or platform compatibility requirement.

.. note::
  NOTE 5: Inclusion of a cryptographic mechanism under the interoperability-based criterion does not classify that mechanism as state of the art cryptography.

EXAMPLE: Examples of product functions of an operating system include secure communication based on TLS 1.3; authenticated encryption of stored or communicated data based on AES-GCM; storage confidentiality based on AES-XTS; kernel module and boot signature verification based on Ed25519 or ECDSA; protection of stored credentials based on Argon2id; and key derivation based on HKDF.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2
TITLE: Assessment of product cryptographic configuration

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.0
TITLE: General

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment in this clause verifies the cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration against clause K.1.1.

For ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms, the assessment verifies that the cryptographic mechanism is either listed in clause K.3.2 or fulfils the criteria specified in clause K.1.1 item 2)b). For interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms, the assessment verifies that the cryptographic mechanism is listed in clause K.4.2. The assessment also verifies that the product uses the cryptographic mechanism in accordance with the relevant characteristics, product functions, use cases where applicable, external specifications or external requirements, and conditions specified in the present document.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.1
TITLE: Assessment of ACM-listed cryptographic mechanisms

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.1.1
TITLE: Assessment objective

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The purpose of this assessment case is to verify that cryptographic mechanisms claimed to comply with clause K.1.1 item 1) and used in the product's default configuration are listed in the ACM catalogue [1].
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.1.2
TITLE: Assessment preparation

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* Preconditions for the assessment: the product's default configuration shall be used for the assessment.

* The documentation shall provide a list of cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration and claimed under clause K.1.1 item 1), including the related product function, relevant parameters where applicable, and the relevant ACM entry.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.1.3
TITLE: Assessment activities

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment shall include verification that:

1) each cryptographic mechanism claimed under clause K.1.1 item 1) is identified by reference to a relevant ACM entry;

2) the ACM entry corresponds to the cryptographic mechanism used in the product's default configuration, including relevant parameters where applicable;

3) where the ACM entry includes lifecycle information, such as expiry date, deprecation date, migration condition or usage limitation, this information is reflected in the documentation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.1.4
TITLE: Assessment evidence

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment evidence shall include, as applicable:

1) description of the product's default configuration;

2) list of cryptographic mechanisms claimed under clause K.1.1 item 1);

3) references to the relevant ACM entries;

4) relevant parameters, profiles, cipher suites or configuration constraints, where applicable;

5) relevant lifecycle information from the ACM catalogue, where applicable.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.1.5
TITLE: Assessment verdict

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* The verdict PASS shall be assigned if the required evidence has been provided, is complete, is applicable to the assessed configuration, and demonstrates compliance with clause K.1.1 item 1).

* The verdict FAIL shall be assigned otherwise.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.2
TITLE: Assessment of ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.2.1
TITLE: Assessment objective

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The purpose of this assessment case is to verify that cryptographic mechanisms claimed to comply with clause K.1.1 item 2) and used in the product's default configuration are either listed as ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms in clause K.3.2 or fulfil the criteria specified in clause K.1.1 item 2.b, and are used for the claimed product function(s).
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.2.2
TITLE: Assessment preparation

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* Preconditions for the assessment: the product's default configuration shall be used for the assessment.

* The documentation shall provide a list of cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration and claimed under clause K.1.1 item 2), including the related product function, use case where applicable, relevant parameters where applicable, and at least one of the following: the relevant entry in clause K.3.2; or evidence that the cryptographic mechanism fulfils all applicable criteria in clause K.1.1 item 2)b).
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.2.3
TITLE: Assessment activities

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment shall include verification that:

1) each cryptographic mechanism claimed under clause K.1.1 item 2) is either listed in clause K.3.2 as an ACM-extended cryptographic mechanism or supported by evidence demonstrating fulfilment of the applicable criteria in clause K.1.1 item 2)b);

2) the cryptographic mechanism used by the product corresponds to the mechanism listed in clause K.3.2 or described in the evidence provided under clause K.1.1 item 2)b);

3) the product function using the cryptographic mechanism, and the use case where applicable, correspond to the related product function and use case specified in clause K.3.2 or justified under clause K.1.1 item 2)b);

4) the relevant characteristics, parameters, profiles, cipher suites or configuration constraints of the cryptographic mechanism correspond to those specified in clause K.3.2 or justified under clause K.1.1 item 2)b), where applicable;

5) where clause K.3.2 or the justification under clause K.1.1 item 2)b) defines conditions or limitations for the use of the cryptographic mechanism, these conditions or limitations are reflected in the product's default configuration;

6) where technically feasible, the cryptographic mechanism, parameters and configuration identified in the documentation correspond to the assessed product configuration.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.2.4
TITLE: Assessment evidence

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment evidence shall include, as applicable:

1) description of the product's default configuration;

2) list of cryptographic mechanisms claimed under clause K.1.1 item 2);

3) reference to the relevant entry in clause K.3.2, where the mechanism is listed there;

4) evidence that the cryptographic mechanism fulfils the criteria in clause K.1.1 item 2)b), where compliance is claimed on the basis of those criteria;

5) identification of the related product function using the cryptographic mechanism and the use case where applicable;

6) relevant characteristics, parameters, profiles, cipher suites or configuration constraints, where applicable;

7) evidence that the cryptographic mechanism is used in accordance with the conditions or limitations specified in clause K.3.2 or in the justification under clause K.1.1 item 2)b), where applicable.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.2.5
TITLE: Assessment verdict

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* The verdict PASS shall be assigned if the required evidence has been provided, is complete, is applicable to the assessed configuration, and demonstrates compliance with clause K.1.1 item 2).

* The verdict FAIL shall be assigned otherwise.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

