TITLE: Assessment objective

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The purpose of this assessment case is to verify that cryptographic mechanisms claimed to comply with clause K.1.1 item 3) and used in the product's default configuration are listed as interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms in clause K.4.2 and are used in accordance with the characteristics, product functions, use cases where applicable, external specifications or external requirements, and conditions specified in clause K.4.2.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.3.2
TITLE: Assessment preparation

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* Preconditions for the assessment: the product's default configuration shall be used for the assessment.

* The documentation shall provide a list of cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration and claimed under clause K.1.1 item 3), including the related product function, use case where applicable, relevant parameters where applicable, the external specification or external requirement requiring its use, and the relevant entry in clause K.4.2.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.3.3
TITLE: Assessment activities

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment shall include verification that:

1) each cryptographic mechanism claimed under clause K.1.1 item 3) is listed in clause K.4.2 as an interoperability-based cryptographic mechanism;

2) the cryptographic mechanism used by the product corresponds to the cryptographic mechanism listed in clause K.4.2;

3) the product function using the cryptographic mechanism, and the use case where applicable, correspond to the related product function and use case specified in clause K.4.2;

4) the external specification or external requirement requiring the use of the cryptographic mechanism corresponds to the external specification or external requirement specified in clause K.4.2;

5) the relevant characteristics, parameters, profiles, cipher suites or configuration constraints of the cryptographic mechanism correspond to those specified in clause K.4.2, where applicable;

6) the conditions or limitations specified in clause K.4.2 for the use of the cryptographic mechanism are reflected in the product's default configuration;

7) where technically feasible, the cryptographic mechanism, parameters and configuration identified in the documentation correspond to the assessed product configuration.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.3.4
TITLE: Assessment evidence

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment evidence shall include, as applicable:

1) description of the product's default configuration;

2) list of cryptographic mechanisms claimed under clause K.1.1 item 3);

3) reference to the relevant entry in clause K.4.2;

4) identification of the related product function using the cryptographic mechanism and the use case where applicable;

5) identification of the external specification or external requirement requiring use of the cryptographic mechanism;

6) relevant characteristics, parameters, profiles, cipher suites or configuration constraints, where applicable;

7) evidence that the cryptographic mechanism is used in accordance with the conditions or limitations specified in clause K.4.2.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.1.2.3.5
TITLE: Assessment verdict

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* The verdict PASS shall be assigned if the required evidence has been provided, is complete, is applicable to the assessed configuration, and demonstrates compliance with clause K.1.1 item 3).

* The verdict FAIL shall be assigned otherwise.

.. note::
  NOTE 1: The assessment of the external specification or external requirement itself is outside the scope of this assessment case. The assessment verifies that the external specification or external requirement is identified in clause K.4.2 and that the cryptographic mechanism is used by the product for the related product function.

.. note::
  NOTE 2: Documentation review is a minimum assessment activity under the present annex. Functional or resistance testing activities can be added by the present document where relevant for the product category, product function or cryptographic mechanism.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2
TITLE: Crypto agility

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.1
TITLE: Requirement

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Where the product's default configuration uses a cryptographic mechanism for which the ACM catalogue [1], or the present document, specifies a deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation falling within the intended lifetime of the product, the product shall provide means for addressing the affected cryptographic mechanism by one or more of the following:

a) updating the cryptographic mechanism;

b) using another cryptographic mechanism that complies with clause K.1.1 and is not subject to the relevant deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation;

c) disabling the use of the affected cryptographic mechanism; or

d) limiting the use of the affected product functions accordingly.

EXAMPLE: Where a security mechanism uses a hybrid cryptographic construction, for example a hybrid key-encapsulation mechanism combining a classical and a post-quantum primitive, the lifecycle status of each constituent primitive is relevant to the lifecycle status of the construction. Hybridisation can be used as part of a planned migration strategy where permitted by the ACM catalogue or by the present document. However, hybridisation does not, by itself, extend the recommended usage lifetime of a constituent primitive that has been deprecated.

.. note::
  NOTE 1: Where a hybrid cryptographic construction includes multiple cryptographic primitives, the lifecycle status of each constituent primitive is relevant to the lifecycle status of the construction. Hybridisation is not assumed to mitigate the deprecation of a constituent primitive unless the ACM catalogue or the present document classifies the hybrid construction as suitable for the corresponding product function.

.. note::
  NOTE 2: Crypto agility supports maintaining appropriate cryptographic protection within the intended lifetime of the product, in addition to the capability of updating cryptographic mechanisms on the product in accordance with secure update and secure communication mechanisms.

.. note::
  NOTE 3: Deprecation information from sources other than the ACM catalogue or the present document can be considered as input to vulnerability management or security risk assessment but does not by itself trigger the requirement in clause K.2.1.

.. note::
  NOTE 4: Where the product provides cryptographic capabilities for use by other products, components or layers, the mechanism required by clause K.2.1 can consist of update, configuration, disabling, deprecation or limitation capabilities usable by the integrating product, system operator or user, where applicable.

.. note::
  NOTE 5: The applicability condition of this requirement can express exceptions based on product characteristics, for example a cryptographic implementation based on an immutable hardware co-processor or a hardware-based root of trust, or long-lived silicon used in a long-lived non-updateable environment.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.2
TITLE: Assessment of crypto-agility

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.2.1
TITLE: Assessment objective

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The purpose of this assessment case is to verify whether, where the product's default configuration uses a cryptographic mechanism for which the ACM catalogue [1], or the present document, specifies a deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation falling within the intended lifetime of the product, the product provides means to address the affected cryptographic mechanism in accordance with clause K.2.1.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.2.2
TITLE: Assessment preparation

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* Preconditions for the assessment: the product's default configuration shall be used for the assessment.

The documentation shall identify:

a) the intended lifetime of the product;

b) the cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration;

c) the related product function(s) for each cryptographic mechanism;

d) the lifecycle information applicable to each cryptographic mechanism, where specified by the ACM catalogue or the present document, including any deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.2.3
TITLE: Assessment activities

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment shall include verification that:

a) for each cryptographic mechanism used in the product's default configuration, the lifecycle information specified by the ACM catalogue or the present document has been identified, where such information is specified;

b) where the ACM catalogue or the present document specifies a deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation for a cryptographic mechanism, this information is reflected in the documentation;

c) where a deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation falls within the intended lifetime of the product, the product provides an applicable mechanism for updating the cryptographic mechanism, using another cryptographic mechanism that complies with clause K.1.1 and is not subject to the relevant deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation, disabling the use of the affected cryptographic mechanism, or limiting the use of the affected product functions accordingly;

d) where another cryptographic mechanism is used, that cryptographic mechanism complies with clause K.1.1 and is not subject to the relevant deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.2.4
TITLE: Assessment evidence

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The assessment evidence shall include, as applicable:

a) documentation of the intended lifetime of the product;

b) list of cryptographic mechanisms used in the product's default configuration;

c) identification of the related product function(s) for each cryptographic mechanism;

d) references to the ACM catalogue entry or to the provision of the present document used to determine lifecycle information, where applicable;

e) identification of any deprecation date, expiry date, migration condition or usage limitation falling within the intended lifetime of the product;

f) description of the means provided by the product, such as update of the cryptographic mechanism, use of another cryptographic mechanism that complies with clause K.1.1 and is not subject to the relevant lifecycle constraint, disabling the use of the affected cryptographic mechanism, or limitation of the use of the affected product functions.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.2.2.5
TITLE: Assessment verdict

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
* The verdict PASS shall be assigned if the required evidence has been provided, is complete, is applicable to the assessed configuration, and demonstrates compliance with clause K.2.1.

* The verdict FAIL shall be assigned otherwise.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.3
TITLE: ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.3.1
TITLE: Requirement

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Where the product's default configuration uses ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms, these mechanisms shall comply with the ACM-extended criterion in clause K.1.1 item 2).
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.3.2
TITLE: List of ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms specified by the present document are listed in Table K.3-1.

.. list-table:: Table K.3-1: ACM-extended cryptographic mechanisms specified by the present document.
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 14 14 24 24 24

   * - Cryptographic mechanism
     - Type
     - Related product function
     - Specification
     - Conditions or limitations
   * - ChaCha20
     - Stream cipher
     - Software-based confidentiality, in particular protected communication and volume encryption on platforms without AES hardware acceleration
     - IETF RFC 8439 [9]
     - Used with unique nonces per key. Not used alone where integrity or authenticity is required; in that case used within an authenticated construction or combined with an appropriate integrity mechanism.
   * - ChaCha20-Poly1305
     - Authenticated encryption (AEAD)
     - Confidentiality and data-origin authentication of communicated or stored data, for example TLS, secure tunnels, and software-based encryption where AES acceleration is unavailable
     - IETF RFC 8439 [9]
     - Nonces generated and managed according to the applicable protocol profile. Reuse of a nonce with the same key prevented.
   * - Poly1305
     - Message authentication code
     - Message integrity and data-origin authentication, primarily as part of ChaCha20-Poly1305
     - IETF RFC 8439 [9]
     - Not used as a standalone MAC unless the implementation ensures correct one-time key generation and use according to IETF RFC 8439 [9].
   * - X25519 and X448
     - Elliptic-curve Diffie-Hellman key agreement
     - Key agreement for TLS, SSH and secure-channel establishment
     - IETF RFC 7748 [6]; IETF RFC 8410 [8] for X.509 algorithm identifiers
     - The shared secret is processed through a suitable key derivation function or protocol-defined key schedule. Used only in protocol profiles that provide peer authentication and protection against active attacks where required.
   * - Ed25519 and Ed448
     - Digital signature (EdDSA)
     - Signature verification for kernel module signing, boot integrity, software update and package signing, and SSH
     - IETF RFC 8032 [7]; IETF RFC 8410 [8] for X.509 algorithm identifiers
     - Used where supported by the applicable certificate profile, update framework or trust framework, with an appropriate key-management model. Ed25519 and Ed448 provide signature verification, not entity authentication by themselves.
   * - Argon2id
     - Password hashing function
     - Protection of stored user and administrator authentication credentials (password verification), and derivation of storage-encryption keys from passphrases where the product implements passphrase-protected storage encryption
     - IETF RFC 9106 [10]
     - Each password verifier uses a unique random salt of at least 128 bits. Memory, iteration and parallelism parameters selected according to the product threat model and documented. Where used as a key derivation function for storage encryption, the salt is not reused across volumes or derived keys.
   * - yescrypt
     - Password hashing function
     - Protection of stored authentication credentials and derivation of keys from passwords
     - Leverages IETF RFC 7914 [11] with state-of-the-art enhancements for GPU attack and cache-timing side-channel resistance. Uses ACM and NIST-approved cryptographic primitives: SHA-256 (ACM 2.3, FIPS PUB 180-4 [21]), HMAC (ACM 3.3, FIPS PUB 198-1 [22]), and PBKDF2 (ACM 3.7, NIST SP 800-132 [14]). It was a finalist with special recognition in the Password Hashing Competition (2013-2015) and is recommended in ANSSI's Guidelines for GNU/Linux Systems [i.14].
     - Each password verifier uses a unique random salt of at least 128 bits. An output of at least 256 bits. Cost parameter defaulting to 5 or greater.
   * - SP 800-108 KDFs
     - Key Derivation Function
     - Derivation of subordinate keys from an existing secret key
     - NIST SP 800-108 Rev. 1 [3]
     - Derives additional keying material from an already established secret key using an approved PRF (e.g. HMAC, CMAC, or KMAC). Security depends on the strength of the source key and the approved PRF.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.3.3
TITLE: Assessment

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Compliance with the ACM-extended criterion in clause K.1.1 item) 2 is assessed in clause K.1.2.2.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.4
TITLE: Interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.4.1
TITLE: Requirement

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Where the product's default configuration uses interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms, these mechanisms shall be selected from the interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms listed in clause K.4.2 for the specified product functions and external specifications or external requirements, and shall be used in accordance with the conditions or limitations specified in clause K.4.2.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.4.2
TITLE: List of interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
The interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms specified by the present document are listed in Table K.4-1. Inclusion of a cryptographic mechanism in this clause does not classify that mechanism as state of the art cryptography.

.. list-table:: Table K.4-1: Interoperability-based cryptographic mechanisms specified by the present document
   :header-rows: 1
   :widths: 12 12 18 16 21 21

   * - Cryptographic mechanism
     - Type
     - Related product function
     - Specification
     - Interoperability justification
     - Conditions or limitations
   * - scrypt
     - Password hashing function
     - Protection of stored authentication credentials, or derivation of keys from passwords, where an existing format requires it
     - IETF RFC 7914 [11]
     - Required by an identified external specification, stored password-verifier format, encrypted storage format, or established deployment profile
     - Use limited to the identified interoperability purpose. Each password verifier uses a unique random salt of at least 128 bits. Cost parameters N, r and p selected and justified according to the product threat model and intended lifetime. Inclusion under this route does not classify scrypt as state of the art cryptography.
   * - MD5
     - Hash function
     - Detection of data corruption
     - IETF RFC 1321 [12]
     - Widely used and deeply embedded in product ecosystems as a checksum for non-adversarial purposes
     - Used only for checksum purposes. Inclusion under this route does not classify MD5 as state of the art cryptography.
   * - SHA-1
     - Hash function
     - Detection of data corruption
     - IETF RFC 3174 [13]
     - Widely used and deeply embedded in product ecosystems as a checksum for non-adversarial purposes
     - Used only for checksum purposes. Inclusion under this route does not classify SHA-1 as state of the art cryptography.
   * - DES/TDEA
     - Symmetric Block Cipher
     - Legacy data encryption, compatibility with older protocols and file formats
     - FIPS PUB 46-3 [i.13]
     - Required to access or decrypt legacy data, systems, or protocols that cannot yet be migrated to stronger algorithms
     - Retained only for decrypting or accessing legacy data protected with DES/TDEA.
   * - RC2
     - Symmetric Block Cipher
     - Legacy certificate, S/MIME, and PKCS#12 compatibility scenarios
     - IETF RFC 2268 [15]
     - Required for import, export, or processing of legacy certificates, archives, and encrypted content created by older products and infrastructure
     - Retained only for decrypting or accessing legacy data protected with RC2.
   * - RC4
     - Symmetric Stream Cipher
     - Legacy TLS, SSL, Kerberos, and application compatibility scenarios
     - IETF RFC 7465 [16]; IETF RFC 6229 [17]
     - Required only to communicate with legacy clients, servers, or applications that have not yet migrated to modern cipher suites. Used solely for backward compatibility
     - Supported only for backward compatibility with legacy data or protocols.
   * - NIST P-192 (secp192r1)
     - Elliptic Curve Cryptography (ECC)
     - Legacy ECDSA signature verification and ECDH key agreement interoperability
     - FIPS PUB 186-5 [18]; SEC 2 [19]
     - Required to interoperate with legacy PKI deployments, certificates, smart cards, embedded devices, and network appliances that continue to use P-192 for authentication or key agreement
     - Retained for processing existing certificates, signatures, and protocols that depend on P-192.
   * - SP 800-135
     - Key Derivation Function
     - Derivation of session encryption, integrity and authentication keys
     - NIST SP 800-135 Rev. 1 [20]
     - Required for interoperability with standards compliant SSH servers and client. Enables secure remote administration, file transfer, and automation scenarios using SSH protocols. Required to interoperate with standards-compliant TLS implementations and device session keys necessary for secure communications between clients and servers
     - Retained only for accessing legacy protocols. Where the product is in control of crypto agility, the product shall default to state-of-the art cryptography.
<<<

[[/SECTION]]

[[SECTION]]
LEVEL: K.4.3
TITLE: Assessment

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Compliance with the requirement in clause K.4 is assessed in clause K.1.2.3.

.. note::
  NOTE: The assessment of the external specifications or external requirements themselves is outside the scope of this assessment. Their relevance is deemed confirmed by their inclusion in the present document.
<<<

